"""Original end-to-end designs. All numerical inputs are teaching assumptions."""
CASES={}
def case(n,raw,flow,entities,code):
    sections=[p.strip().replace(" ¶ ","\n\n") for p in raw.strip().split("\n\n")]
    assert len(sections)==22,(n,len(sections))
    CASES[n]=dict(sections=sections,flow=flow,entities=entities,code=code)

case(1, '''A user submits a long URL and receives a short code. Later, a visitor opens that code and is redirected to the original target. The owner can expire or revoke the link and manage it through an authenticated interface. ¶ Keep the original target and creation identity. Click analytics is secondary work: a failed analytics database should not make an otherwise valid redirect wait indefinitely. Abuse handling and the correctness of the redirect remain part of the main product.

For this example, target redirect p99 below 100 ms under the supported peak. An acknowledged mapping must survive the selected single-storage-node failure, and management operations must preserve ownership. ¶ Specify how soon revocation and expiry take effect. A long-lived cached redirect can remain usable after the source changes unless another mechanism prevents it. The latency target, storage acknowledgment policy, and cache freshness rule therefore need to be checked together rather than promised independently.

Assume 100 creations/s and 10,000 redirects/s on average, with a tenfold redirect peak. Assume 200 stored bytes per mapping before indexes and extra copies. Daily raw growth is 100×86,400×200 = 1.728 GB. ¶ For transfer calculations, assume a 500-byte redirect response. These quantities are teaching inputs. Actual record size, traffic duration, and transport overhead must be measured before using them to provision a service.

Begin with one web application, one relational mapping table, and a unique code constraint. A background job handles analytics. Local uniqueness is straightforward, and this arrangement is suitable for an initial small workload. ¶ Its weaknesses are visible: the application host can fail, and every miss reaches the one data path. Measure lookup and write behaviour before distributing data. A sharded design adds routing and uniqueness work that may not yet solve the actual bottleneck.

POST /links accepts a target, expiry, and idempotency key. GET /{code} returns the permitted redirect or the defined expired/revoked result. DELETE /links/{id} checks the owner before changing state. ¶ Validate supported URL schemes and syntax without fetching arbitrary destinations. Reusing a key with different content conflicts; retrying an identical creation can recover its original result. State whether redirects are permanent or temporary, since that affects how clients may keep copies after later changes.

A link record contains id, unique code, target, owner_id, creation time, expiry, state, and version. An operation record binds the owner and operation key to a request fingerprint and result link. The key is unique within the owner scope. ¶ The code must be unique across its intended namespace. If mappings are later sharded, routing or allocation must preserve that rule across owners. A uniqueness constraint applied independently to unrelated shards is insufficient. Keep revocation and expiry attached to authoritative versioned state.

LinkService checks the target and caller. CodeAllocator proposes a code. LinkRepository commits the mapping and its operation result together. RedirectResolver decides whether the link is usable, and AnalyticsPublisher records secondary events. ¶ The allocator can encode an appropriately unique integer in base62 or propose random codes and retry collisions. The repository's uniqueness check is the decisive boundary. A random proposal is not proof of uniqueness, and a provider-specific storage error should not leak through every application caller.

Put an edge or reverse proxy before replaceable application instances. A cache serves representations whose expiry and revocation policy permits reuse. The authoritative replicated store owns mappings and recovery; a queue or log carries analytics separately. ¶ The cache can improve reads but must not become the only recoverable copy. Bound fallback traffic when it is unavailable. Extra application instances protect some serving capacity, while storage placement and acknowledgment determine which mapping history survives a failure.

For creation, validate the request, atomically commit the mapping and deduplication result, then reply. For redirect, check usable state, obtain a permitted cached or source value, and send the response. Record analytics independently of the visitor's required result. ¶ Combine concurrent misses for a code where useful. Keep private management representations out of the public redirect cache. The write, read, and analytics paths have different responsibilities and should remain distinguishable in the implementation.

With an assumed 90% hit ratio, 10,000 redirects/s cause 1,000 source reads/s before retries or coalescing. At 100,000 redirects/s, the same ratio implies 10,000 source reads/s. Response traffic is 100,000×500 = 50 MB/s before transport overhead. ¶ Also test a cold cache, when the nominal miss calculation no longer applies. The source and admission policy need a defined response to that workload; ordinary warm-cache success does not establish it.

Assume a warm path spends 20 ms at the edge/network, 10 ms in the application, 5 ms in cache access, and 20 ms returning the result. The sum is 55 ms. ¶ This is a proposed budget, not measured p99. A miss adds source lookup and possible queueing. Measure the tail under hot links, cache loss, storage failover, and required revocation checks before claiming the 100 ms objective. Adding average stage times does not by itself establish an end-to-end percentile.

Two creations propose the same code. The second insert must fail atomically and retry, preserving the first target. A lost creation reply should recover the stored operation result instead of creating another mapping. ¶ Cache outage needs origin admission controls. A stale cache can redirect a revoked link unless the freshness rule prevents it. Analytics failure should affect analytics progress rather than valid redirect service. Test these separate histories; one successful create-and-open demonstration leaves all of them unexamined.

Add application capacity when independent application work is limiting. Add shared or edge caching when measured reads justify it. Partition mappings only when the authoritative storage path needs division. ¶ A globally popular code may need permitted read copies instead of more cold partitions. Every added copy retains the expiry and revocation obligation. Data movement needs routing, version, and recovery rules, so expansion should preserve both global code ownership and the supported state of existing operations.

Creating a code needs one atomic uniqueness decision. The owner-facing read should meet the chosen read-after-write rule. Public redirects may tolerate delayed target changes only within the stated expiry and revocation contract. ¶ Analytics can be processed later with stable event identities. These operations therefore have different consistency needs. Calling the whole system “eventually consistent” hides the exclusive code insertion and the permitted delay of a click count. State the observable rule for each path.

Authorise owner management and limit creation abuse. Accept only supported target schemes. If previews fetch destinations, restrict egress and validate redirect hops so a public URL field does not become access to private services. ¶ Protect credentials and avoid leaking private targets through logs or analytics. A short code that is difficult to guess is still a link, not a complete permission system for confidential content. If restricted destinations are supported, define authentication and access checking for those redirects separately.

Start with a region and a clear failure contract. Use compatible rolling application releases, a suitably replicated authoritative store, and tested backups. Preserve operation identities through replacement and drain connections as needed. ¶ New code must understand existing mapping versions. A later second region needs a deliberate write-authority and possible-loss policy. Add it for a named locality or recovery requirement, rather than assuming that more geography automatically preserves every acknowledged mapping.

Measure redirect outcomes and latency, separating hits from misses. Track origin admission, hot codes, code conflicts, storage progress, and how long revocation takes to reach permitted readers. ¶ Observe analytics lag separately. A good cache hit ratio does not show whether a newly created or revoked link is correct. Alert on useful request failures and rapid budget consumption, and use traces or sampled histories to locate the affected path without exposing private target data unnecessarily.

Model compute as instance-hours times unit rate, storage as retained bytes times copy factor and rate, and transfer as response bytes times rate. Keep the duration assumption explicit. ¶ For example, sustaining 100,000 redirects/s for 30 days at 500 bytes produces 129.6 TB before overhead. That is a different workload from a brief peak of the same height. Use actual traffic durations and current rates for spending decisions; these formulas only expose the inputs the estimate requires.

Allocated sequential identifiers simplify code generation but need a valid allocator and can reveal order. Random codes reduce that predictability but still need collision detection. Neither option grants access control. ¶ Permanent redirects can let clients reuse responses more aggressively, making later changes harder to enforce. Temporary redirects retain more server control at a traffic cost. Compare the alternatives using revocation, mutation, privacy, and load requirements. The right choice is the smallest mechanism that preserves those meanings, not the most familiar coding trick.

Use traffic levels as teaching checkpoints rather than hardware promises. Around 100 requests/s, begin with one application and transactional store. Around 1,000, inspect plans and indexes. Around 10,000, consider measured application and cache needs. ¶ At 100,000, expand the actual bottleneck and verify cold-cache and degraded operation. A given machine may reach a limit earlier or later. Preserve stable code and operation identities at every stage so scaling does not change the meaning of existing links or retries.

The main uncertainties are revocation freshness, source capacity after cache loss, and global uniqueness during partitioning. Pair each with a specific check: revoke under load, empty or disable the cache, and force a collision across the real ownership boundary. ¶ Record acceptable degraded outcomes and unresolved evidence. Replicating the cache alone does not answer these questions. A useful review ends with the failure behaviour the design supports and the test that could show its assumption wrong.

Implement base62 encoding and a repository with an atomic unique-code constraint. Force two proposals to use one code and check that the original target remains unchanged. ¶ Then stop execution after commit but before returning the creation result. Retry with the same owner and operation key. The expected result is the existing mapping, with no second logical creation. Also check that changing the target under that key returns the documented conflict.''',
['Client creates link', 'Atomic mapping + operation commit', 'Resolver reads cache or source', 'Redirect; analytics deferred'],
['Owner 1 → many Links', 'Link code is UNIQUE', 'Operation key → one result', 'Analytics events are derived'],
'def allocate(target, operation_key):\n    prior = operations.find(operation_key)\n    if prior: return prior\n    while attempts_remaining():\n        code = propose_code()\n        try:\n            with transaction():\n                link = insert_unique_code(code, target)\n                save_operation_result(operation_key, link.id)\n            return link\n        except CodeConflict:\n            continue\n        except OperationConflict:\n            return load_and_validate_prior_result(operation_key, target)\n    raise AllocationUnavailable')

case(2, '''A request arrives from a caller and asks to perform work with a known cost. The limiter decides whether the current policy permits it and returns useful guidance if it must wait or be rejected. ¶ Distinguish continuing rate, permitted burst, and cumulative quota. For a distributed deployment, state whether the limit is exact globally or a bounded regional approximation. Giving every region a full independent global bucket silently creates a different policy.

Admission should add little delay while keeping protected resources within their allowance. Preserve tenant fairness and specify how the limiter behaves when its state is unavailable. ¶ An exact global cap can require rejection or waiting during a partition. Failing open cannot preserve that cap unless previously allocated safe budgets cover the admitted work. Choose the failure rule alongside the availability goal. “Always fast and always exact” needs a protocol and assumptions, rather than two independent targets.

Assume 100,000 checks/s and one million active keys. A sample policy holds ten tokens and refills two per second. At an assumed 64 bytes of raw state per key, the raw total is 64 MB. ¶ Engine allocation, expiry metadata, replication, and network requests add to this. These are teaching inputs, not product benchmarks. State size is only one capacity dimension; the update rate and contention of a popular key may become limiting sooner.

Begin with a dictionary in one process and protect each bucket transition with a suitable lock. This provides one local atomic decision for the callers sharing that process. ¶ Starting a second unrelated process gives it another full allowance. Restart may reset the bucket too. The simple version is appropriate only if this scope and reset behaviour meet the requirement. Demonstrating correct arithmetic locally does not establish a shared cap across instances.

check(key,cost,policy_version) returns the admission decision, remaining allowance, and meaningful retry delay. Derive the key from trusted caller and resource identity; a caller must not evade its policy by inventing another key. ¶ Require a positive bounded cost. Policy administration needs authorisation and an effective-version rule. Distinguish overload from invalid business input, and state whether rejected attempts consume a separate abuse budget. These meanings let callers react without immediately recreating the same excessive demand.

Keep a bucket record with scope_key, policy_version, tokens, last_refill, and expiry. Refill and spending share one atomic update. A cumulative daily quota can require separate state and rules. ¶ Fixed-point or integer units can avoid unnecessary numeric drift. A policy change needs defined treatment of existing tokens and capacity. The elapsed-time basis must be trusted; client timestamps must not let a caller refill early. Expiring state also changes allowance, so it belongs in the contract.

KeyResolver derives trusted scope, PolicyResolver supplies validated policy, and Clock supplies the time basis. BucketStore performs the coordinated transition. AdmissionService translates the result for the caller. ¶ These roles make deterministic tests practical, but do not replace atomic state access. Two pure calculations using the same old token count can both admit work. Keep the refill/check/debit decision under one owner or supported transaction. The decisive property concerns the combined operation, not merely each helper's correctness.

Use coarse admission near ingress and more specific protection before expensive resources. Independent keys can have different owners. A strict shared bucket needs one suitable atomic authority or a reviewed distributed protocol. ¶ Regional budgets can avoid a remote check on each request, but need bounded allocation and replenishment. State the allowed overshoot or outstanding-budget rule. Protect the limiter's own capacity and define emergency controls. A protection service can otherwise become the first component overloaded by the traffic it was meant to regulate.

Derive the request's scope and current policy. Under coordinated access, load its bucket, calculate nonnegative elapsed time, refill no further than capacity, compare the cost, and debit only if allowed. ¶ Compute retry delay from the deficit and refill rate when that advice is meaningful. Record bounded metrics. Splitting the read and final write into unprotected calls lets two requests spend the same last token. The atomic transition is what connects correct arithmetic to a correct concurrent decision.

One million 64-byte records require 64 MB raw. A remote check per incoming request also means 100,000 limiter requests/s under the assumed workload, before replica work and retries. ¶ A single hot key can serialise many updates despite evenly distributed storage. Measure check rate, active state, contention, and replicated write cost. Batching or local budgets may reduce traffic, but their changed timing and precision must be stated. A memory estimate alone is not a complete limiter capacity plan.

Assume a 100 ms application target and allocate 5 ms to admission. This is a budget to validate under contention, not a measured guarantee. ¶ A strict cross-region decision adds propagation and queueing and may not fit it. Local preallocated budgets can reduce that delay while adding an allocation protocol. Measure the complete path, including store failover and policy retrieval, against the required precision. Changing the consistency contract can change latency, so present those choices together.

Two requests read one token and both pass. Repair that race by combining the read, refill, and debit atomically. Backward time must not create extra refill, and state expiry must not grant an unintended unsafe burst. ¶ Test key spoofing and policy disagreement as well as arithmetic. During owner failure, apply the documented reject, safe-local-budget, or bounded fail-open policy. A mathematically correct bucket can still fail to protect the resource if identities or deployment scopes are wrong.

Partition independent keys while keeping a bucket's decision coordinated. Cache versioned policy definitions where suitable, while maintaining the authority needed for spending. ¶ One strict hot bucket cannot be divided freely without a budget-allocation protocol. Hierarchical limits or local allowances can help when their semantics fit the resource. Different operations may deserve different costs. Scale according to protected work and measured limiter contention, rather than increasing the key count while leaving the hot decision unchanged.

A strict bucket preserves one policy's refill-and-debit rule under its time model. An approximate distributed version needs a stated bound, such as the total independently outstanding regional allowance. ¶ Token buckets permit bursts. Fixed windows have boundary effects, while sliding windows model recent history differently. These are different admission contracts. Converging usage metrics later cannot repair requests already admitted beyond a mandatory cap. Name the invariant the live decision must preserve instead of treating eventual counter agreement as equivalent protection.

Bind scope keys to authenticated tenant or service identity. Guard policy changes and cap key creation so arbitrary callers cannot exhaust state memory. Do not trust client time, policy names, or tenant headers without validation. ¶ Limit what usage information responses reveal about other callers. Admission and permission remain separate: possessing tokens does not authorise a read of another tenant's document. Apply the resource access rule after, or as part of, the relevant protected operation.

Deploy the policy and bucket owners with specified state-loss and failover behaviour. A restart that grants every caller a new full bucket changes the protection contract. ¶ Roll out policy versions compatibly and test time behaviour, old clients, and shared-owner loss. A local fallback must have an acceptable bound. Preserve state or allocate safe replacement allowance according to the chosen design, rather than relying on the assumption that protection state is disposable merely because individual records are small.

Measure allowed and rejected cost, check latency, owner errors, active keys, hot-key pressure, and policy-version differences. Compare these with the resource the limiter protects. ¶ Many rejections can indicate effective protection or a bad configuration blocking legitimate users. Few rejections can hide fail-open behaviour during store failure. Observe both protection failures and unnecessary denial. Use bounded dimensions for metrics so tenant or key cardinality does not make the monitoring system the next capacity problem.

The cost includes state memory, check traffic, replicated updates, transfer, and operating work. A remote strict decision on every cheap business request can cost more than the operation being protected. ¶ Compare strict and local-budget designs with their guarantees stated. Use symbolic or explicitly fictional rates for teaching. If the cheaper choice permits more overshoot or fails differently, show that difference. A reduced bill does not establish equal protection when the policy has changed.

Token bucket admits bounded bursts. A leaky-bucket shaper smooths output by adding waiting. Fixed-window counters are simple but permit bursts around boundaries. Sliding approaches model recent traffic at additional state or computation cost. ¶ Regional budgets trade precision and allocation work for local delay and availability. Choose according to the protected resource and acceptable behaviour, not a universally preferred algorithm name. Include what a caller sees when rejected and how its retries interact with the limit.

First build a deterministic local bucket with an atomic owner. Move to shared state when several processes need one cap. Partition keys only when measured load requires it. ¶ Introduce regional allocation for a named latency or failure need, with an explicit bound. Preserve the original policy meaning through each step or version the changed contract. Evolution is safest when each addition answers a demonstrated scope or capacity problem rather than silently multiplying the allowance.

Ask whether the advertised scope matches enforcement, whether a policy update creates unintended tokens, and what happens to the hottest key. These are protocol questions beyond the basic refill formula. ¶ Test simultaneous spending of the last token, backward time, expiry, policy replacement, owner failure, and regional reconnection. Reject an exact-global claim implemented as full independent buckets. Record unresolved evidence instead of assuming a shared product name establishes all these behaviours.

Use an injected monotonic clock to build the sample bucket. Spend its ten initial tokens, reject an immediate eleventh spend, advance three seconds, and verify that six tokens have returned under capacity ten. ¶ Reject invalid costs and ensure rollback in elapsed time adds no allowance. Then reproduce two unprotected readers spending one token and fix it with a coordinated transition. The test should show both the incorrect history and why the corrected ownership permits only one winner.''',
['Trusted request key and cost', 'Atomic refill and debit', 'Allow or reject under policy', 'Observe protection of downstream'],
['Policy version defines bucket', 'Scope key owns one state', 'Region budget has explicit bound', 'Quota is a separate lifecycle'],
'def spend(bucket, now, cost):\n    require(cost > 0)\n    elapsed = max(0, now - bucket.last)\n    available = min(bucket.capacity,\n                    bucket.tokens + elapsed * bucket.rate)\n    allowed = available >= cost\n    bucket.tokens = available - cost if allowed else available\n    bucket.last = max(bucket.last, now)\n    return allowed\n# Entire transition executes atomically for this bucket.')

case(3, '''Two people send messages in an authorised conversation. Online devices should receive updates, and a disconnected device should later fetch what it missed. The system also supports bounded groups and a defined retention policy. ¶ Name the receipts carefully. A message can be accepted, persisted, delivered to a device, and read by a person at different times. Presence is only an approximate indication of connectivity. Opening a WebSocket supplies a live path, not evidence of human reading or durable history.

Retain acknowledged messages under the chosen storage-failure contract and protect conversation privacy. Define ordering per conversation or state a deliberately weaker alternative. Target online delivery below one second for the assumed supported workload. ¶ A disconnected client catches up later, so its delivery delay differs from that target. Presence can expire approximately without being treated as a strict allocation invariant. Moderation, deletion, and offline history need their own retention and visibility rules, including what returning devices may fetch.

Assume 100,000 concurrent connections and 10,000 accepted messages/s. Each stored message averages 500 bytes and produces five recipient deliveries in the simplified workload. ¶ Raw ingestion is 5 MB/s, or 432 GB/day. Five deliveries per message produce about 25 MB/s of body traffic before framing and overhead. Model device multiplicity, large groups, connection memory, and reconnect bursts separately. These assumptions do not establish the capacity of a particular gateway or database.

Begin with one connection server and one transactional history store. Save a message before returning its persistence receipt, then attempt delivery to online clients. ¶ A gateway crash loses live connections but need not lose committed history. Clients reconnect and request missing messages. This first design makes ownership clear, while exposing the server's availability and capacity limits. A database commit establishes persistence under its contract; it does not establish that any particular device received the message.

A send operation includes conversation_id, client_message_id, and bounded content. A history request continues after a sequence, for example GET /conversations/{id}/messages?after=sequence. ¶ Receipts identify a device or user and the highest relevant delivered or read sequence under the product's policy. Check membership on reads and writes. Reusing a client message identifier within its sender scope returns the earlier message. This lets a client recover a lost send reply without adding another history record for the same logical message.

Keep conversations and membership separately from message records. A message includes conversation_id, sequence, sender, client_message_id, body, and version. Receipt records track delivered and read progress for the appropriate user or device. ¶ Enforce the intended client-identity uniqueness scope. Presence uses a separate expiring heartbeat record. Conversation order needs a sequence authority or a supported conflict model; timestamps from client clocks do not automatically provide a stable total order. State which identity remains valid through reconnect and retry.

ConnectionGateway manages connections. MembershipPolicy checks access. MessageService coordinates identity and persistence, SequenceOwner establishes order, and HistoryRepository serves bounded pages. DeliveryWorker handles committed fan-out. ¶ ReceiptAggregator combines progress without moving backward under the chosen device policy. Large-group work belongs in an explicit bounded operation instead of a long synchronous loop inside the message transaction. These components can begin together in one deployment. Their contracts should make clear which state is durable and which routing information may disappear.

Use replaceable connection gateways and a conversation-routed durable message path. A log or outbox carries delivery intent to workers. Routing metadata identifies currently connected recipient devices, while history storage remains authoritative. ¶ A route can be stale, so clients recover missing frames from their last known sequence. If publication intent commits with the message, a broker outage can delay live delivery without discarding acknowledged history. Gateway state is then a recoverable convenience for reaching a device, rather than the only record of what should be delivered.

The client sends a stable message identity. The service checks membership, assigns order, and commits the message with its delivery intent. It then returns the persistence result. ¶ Workers send live frames and process valid receipts. On reconnect, the client supplies its last observed sequence, fetches missing pages, and detects gaps. Repeated frames are recognised by message identity. This separates two questions: what the service has safely retained and what this device currently displays. Losing a live frame is repaired through history rather than assumed impossible.

At 10,000 messages/s and 500 bytes each, raw storage grows by 5 MB/s, or 432 GB/day. Five recipients imply 50,000 deliveries/s and 25 MB/s of bodies. ¶ Connection memory needs measurement. At an illustrative 20 kB per connection, 100,000 connections require 2 GB before other process memory and queued content. Bound outbound buffers for slow devices. A sender can otherwise make an idle-looking connection server run out of memory while the message database remains within its own limits.

Assume 20 ms ingress, 30 ms for commit coordination, 20 ms in delivery queues, and 80 ms on the recipient path. The sum is 150 ms before rendering. ¶ This budget is not measured online p99. Test hot conversations, congestion, failover, and slow-client buffers. Offline delivery has a different timeline. Keep persistence-receipt delay, device-delivery delay, and human-read delay as distinct indicators. One successful transport response cannot establish all three outcomes.

If the send reply is lost after commit, recognise the client's repeated identity. If a gateway stops, reconnect and recover from history. If a client is too slow, disconnect or reduce its service before buffers consume the gateway's resources. ¶ A revoked membership can race with cached access information, so enforce the defined policy for delivery and history. Reconnect storms need admission and staggered retries. Test these event sequences directly rather than only testing two clients that stay connected throughout a normal exchange.

Distribute independent gateway connections and partition history and ordering by conversation where suitable. Use bounded fan-out workers for groups. ¶ One extremely popular conversation can still concentrate ordering or distribution work. It may need different large-channel semantics rather than forcing every recipient into a low-latency acknowledgment chain. Preserve sequence and deduplication identity during ownership movement. Add capacity for the measured limit, with history catch-up included in the workload. Ordinary message averages do not account for every device returning after a gateway event.

Provide a defined per-conversation order and a sender confirmation that agrees with the stored message. History catch-up must allow a client to resume without silently skipping required records. ¶ Cross-conversation total order is unnecessary unless the product requires it. Frames can repeat, so devices deduplicate. Presence and receipt updates can propagate later, while delivered/read progress follows the chosen monotonic model. State the guarantees at these different boundaries instead of describing WebSocket delivery as exactly-once messaging.

Check membership on send, history, fan-out, search, and media access. Limit abusive sending, group size, and payloads. Avoid putting message bodies into routine telemetry. ¶ If end-to-end encryption is required, design device keys, recovery, and membership changes, and explain the resulting limits on server features. TLS protects a connection but does not implement that product guarantee. File attachments need access and retention rules too. Treat each content path as part of conversation privacy rather than assuming an authorised chat page protects every derived URL.

Drain gateways during rollout and support reconnecting old clients. Evolve message and receipt formats compatibly, and preserve history according to the selected replication and promotion rules. ¶ Load-test reconnect and catch-up alongside normal sending. Restarting every gateway together can generate a larger burst than ordinary chat traffic. A new region also needs explicit ordering and membership behaviour. State how existing client identities and progress survive replacement so deployment does not turn uncertain sends into duplicated messages.

Measure persistence delay, live-delivery lag, missing sequence recovery, reconnect rate, fan-out backlog, and hot conversations. Observe slow-client limits and membership denials. ¶ Correlate client and server message identities in suitable detailed evidence, without creating an unbounded metric label for every message. Synthetic clients can send, disconnect, reconnect, and check history and forbidden access. This tests the complete journey. A healthy connection count alone cannot show that an accepted message reached history or that a revoked device is blocked.

Separate connection capacity, message history, fan-out transfer, indexes, replicas, media, and telemetry. Under the assumptions, thirty days of raw messages need 432 GB/day×30 = 12.96 TB before indexes and copies. ¶ Media can dominate storage and transfer and needs its own quota and lifecycle. Use explicit rates or symbolic calculations rather than assumed provider prices. Cost per retained message and cost per delivered body explain different work, particularly when many recipients or devices multiply the live path.

WebSockets supports bidirectional low-latency messaging with connection-management work. SSE with HTTP sends may fit some clients; polling is simpler but creates requests during idle periods. ¶ A single conversation sequence authority simplifies order while creating a hot-key boundary. Conflict-tolerant regional writing changes that contract and needs a suitable merge and ordering model. Compare alternatives against the actual offline, receipt, and privacy requirements. The least complex transport that meets those behaviours can be preferable to one offering features the product never uses.

First make durable history and reconnect recovery correct. Add gateway replicas with explicit connection routing. Introduce durable fan-out for higher volume or groups, then partition conversation ownership when storage or ordering measurements justify it. ¶ Regional placement comes with another authority and failure decision. Preserve message identity at every step. A client retry should retain the same logical meaning after a shard move or gateway replacement. Evolution that loses this identity can duplicate messages even while increasing connection capacity.

Review the meanings of “sent” and “delivered,” the hottest conversation, slow-client memory, and revocation. Each needs a specific supported history. ¶ Test commit followed by a lost send reply, a dropped frame, gateway loss, a stale route, and a history read after membership is removed. Confirm the resulting list and receipts. A demo with two permanent online connections proves little about catch-up, privacy during change, or the storage contract that makes messages durable.

Build a client model that recognises message identities and detects sequence gaps. Save a message but suppress its live frame. Reconnect from the earlier sequence and verify that history supplies it once in the visible list. ¶ Repeat the same client_message_id and check that storage does not add another logical message. Distinguish deduplicating displayed frames from deduplicating history creation: both are useful, and each protects a different boundary.''',
['Client sends stable message ID', 'Conversation owner commits sequence', 'Delivery fans out to devices', 'Reconnect reads durable history'],
['Conversation 1 → many Messages', 'Membership authorizes participant', 'Message has client dedup identity', 'Receipt tracks monotonic progress'],
'with transaction():\n    existing = find_by_client_id(sender, client_message_id)\n    if existing: return existing\n    require_member(sender, conversation)\n    seq = allocate_conversation_sequence(conversation)\n    msg = insert_message(conversation, seq, body)\n    insert_outbox(msg.id, "deliver")\nreturn persisted_receipt(msg)\n# Device delivery and read receipt happen later.')

case(4, '''An application submits a notification intent. The service selects permitted channels, renders a versioned template, sends through email, SMS, or push, and tracks what happened. Some messages may be scheduled or cancelled within a defined window. ¶ Provider acceptance, device delivery, and human viewing are separate outcomes. Preference rules can also differ by message purpose. Keep that policy explicit rather than letting a channel adapter decide whether a particular user may receive an operational or product message.

Preserve accepted intents and make delayed, expired, or permanently failed work visible. Define a business expiry: a password-reset message delivered hours late may be useless or harmful. ¶ Isolate critical and bulk work so a marketing batch does not consume every delivery resource. Avoid duplicate relevant effects where provider contracts support that promise, and expose residual uncertainty where they do not. The service should state the outcome it can establish rather than equating a submitted request with a delivered message.

Assume ten million intents/day. Dividing by 86,400 gives about 115.7/s average. An assumed tenfold peak is about 1,157/s. At 1 kB of initial intent and attempt metadata per item, raw growth is 10 GB/day. ¶ Repeated attempts, indexes, copies, and retained content add costs. Channel quotas are separate configured inputs that must be checked with the actual provider. An overall average cannot establish the submission capacity or delay of a specific route.

A first version calls one provider directly after a user action. It is simple, but the user's request now waits for that provider. If the business action commits and the process dies before sending, the notification intent can disappear. ¶ Persist required intent in the business transaction or an outbox, then let a worker send it. This separates accepted work from a temporary provider problem. It adds queued state and recovery rules, which should be visible rather than hidden inside an untracked background call.

POST /notifications accepts the recipient, template version, variables, channel policy, expiry, and idempotency key. GET /notifications/{id} reports a state such as pending, submitted, delivered, expired, failed, or cancelled. ¶ Define cancellation's boundary: it cannot recall a message already accepted by a provider. Callback endpoints verify authenticity and recognise repeated events. State transitions must reflect provider evidence. A delivered label should not merely mean that a worker returned without an exception.

Store the logical intent, template version, relevant preference information, channel/recipient delivery tasks, attempts, and provider-event inbox. Uniqueness binds each intended effect to its stable identity under the chosen policy. ¶ Keep provider references and uncertain outcomes. Retain sensitive message content separately from minimal progress or audit metadata. Decide what a new preference setting means for already queued tasks. Consent or cancellation rules may require a current check at send time rather than relying only on the submission snapshot.

NotificationService records intent. PreferencePolicy selects allowed channels. TemplateRenderer builds the message, SenderStrategy chooses channel behaviour, and ProviderAdapter maps its protocol and outcomes. DeliveryRepository owns durable attempts and progress. ¶ Metrics decorators and factories can support genuine variation without changing result meanings. Keep construction and resource lifetimes clear. A retry wrapper needs more than the same method name: it must preserve operation identity and account for uncertainty about a prior submission.

Use durable ingress, preference and template access, channel-specific queues, bounded workers, callback inboxes, and an outcome store. Separate resources for critical and bulk traffic where their priorities require it. ¶ A failed provider should occupy only its allocated capacity. A queue helps recovery only when intent, attempts, acknowledgment, and progress records agree about ownership. Draw these state boundaries alongside the normal sending path so it is clear what survives a worker or broker interruption.

Accept and deduplicate the intent, choose permitted channel and template, and persist the delivery task. A worker checks its current authority, expiry, and required preference policy before submission. ¶ Use a stable provider operation identity where supported. Save the returned result or an uncertain state, then acknowledge according to the task protocol. Apply verified callbacks idempotently, with rules preventing an older event from incorrectly reversing progress. An attempt and a callback can arrive out of order, so state transitions need evidence and identity rather than arrival order alone.

Overall average traffic is about 115.7 intents/s, but each channel has its own capacity. Suppose SMS arrivals are 100/s and its route submits 40/s. Backlog grows by 60/s. ¶ Over ten minutes that adds 60×600 = 36,000 waiting submissions if the rates persist. Model retries, recipient fan-out, and expiry too. The queued work may become useless before capacity catches up. Admission, priority, or supported alternate routes must respond to the channel's real limit rather than the service-wide daily average.

Assume acceptance takes 50 ms, queue wait 2 seconds, and provider submission 500 ms. Provider acceptance then occurs around 2.55 seconds after submission to our API. ¶ The short API latency and delivery journey are different measurements. These inputs are illustrative budgets, not measured percentiles. Actual delivery and human viewing depend on further systems and evidence. Do not advertise a 50 ms notification outcome merely because durable acceptance was quick. Track the completion event that matters for the message's purpose.

A worker sends a message, then dies before recording the provider reply. The provider may already have accepted it. Query or use provider idempotency where available; otherwise state the remaining duplicate risk. ¶ Malformed templates need review, outages need bounded retries and expiry, and callbacks need authenticity checks. Reconcile old submitted or uncertain tasks. A second provider does not automatically resolve uncertainty: sending there can create another message if the first provider's effect already occurred.

Distribute independent recipients and channel tasks, with bounds and any required per-recipient order. Add routes only with a deliberate selection, rate, and uncertainty policy. ¶ Extra providers can improve some outage paths while increasing content and reconciliation work. A timeout followed by route switching can produce two messages. Measure the actual channel bottleneck before adding workers, and keep downstream quotas and critical-message capacity visible. Safe progress is the goal, rather than maximum parallel submissions regardless of expiry or duplicate effects.

Persisting intent and the deduplication result needs an atomic local boundary. Task deliveries may repeat, while local transitions recognise stable identities. Provider acceptance and final delivery have separate evidence. ¶ Retain the template version so a retry does not silently send different content. Apply the chosen current-preference policy at the correct boundary. A general exactly-once label would hide the different guarantees supported by email, SMS, and push providers. State which local record or external effect the specific mechanism protects.

Authorise which actor may send which template to which recipients. Validate variables and escape or encode them appropriately for the target channel. Protect recipient data, provider credentials, and callback authenticity. ¶ Avoid leaking tokens or message bodies through logs. Limit intent creation to prevent abuse. Signed links inside messages need an appropriate audience and lifetime; the delivery system should not broaden their capability accidentally. Include adapter-level protections such as preventing unsafe header or content construction.

Deploy workers with durable attempt state and graceful draining. Scale-down must not abandon unrecorded work. Template edits create new versions rather than changing content under an existing retry identity. ¶ Roll task schemas out compatibly and plan credential rotation. Test deployment during a provider outage so accumulated work does not overwhelm the recovering route. Verify that critical-channel and acceptance policies still work while another channel is saturated or being replaced.

Track accepted intents, queue age by channel, expiry, provider submission delay, uncertain outcomes, callbacks, and duplicate recognition. Separate critical and bulk outcomes so an apparently good total cannot hide important delayed messages. ¶ Alert on old unresolved tasks and rapid budget consumption. A growing queue without worker exceptions can still be a delivery incident. Correlate attempts and provider evidence while keeping private content out of general telemetry. Enqueue success is one stage of the journey, not its final outcome.

Include intent and attempt storage, provider-submitted units, workers, callbacks, transfer, and operating effort. Retries can create billable work even when they do not produce a useful delivery. ¶ Model channel_unit_price×submitted_units using explicit inputs. Content length and character encoding may change SMS billing under the provider contract, which must be checked rather than assumed. Compare cost per accepted intent and per relevant successful delivery. They explain different parts of the service and should not be mixed in one denominator.

Direct synchronous sending has little infrastructure but ties the user path to the provider. Durable queued delivery adds recoverable state and a separate delay. Orchestration can make multi-channel escalation explicit; event reactions may distribute coordination but make total progress harder to see. ¶ A backup provider adds another recovery option and another uncertainty boundary. Choose using message expiry, criticality, and the evidence each provider supplies. A solution that eventually sends something is insufficient when the message must be timely, permitted, and meaningfully tracked.

Start with one sender behind a contract. Add durable intent and a worker when required work must survive failure or leave the user's latency path. Add channel strategies and factories for real variation. ¶ Separate critical and bulk resources as volume and harm justify it. Provider failover and escalation come after the uncertain-outcome policy is explicit. Preserve stable task, template, and attempt identities through these changes, rather than treating scaling as permission to create fresh effects for the same intent.

Review whether “delivered” overstates the evidence, whether expiry and current preferences are checked, and whether failover can duplicate a message. These are product and protocol questions. ¶ Test send-then-crash, forged or repeated callbacks, preference revocation after enqueue, template updates, and channel saturation. Confirm terminal progress does not regress under late events. A dashboard of successful submission requests cannot establish that the intended people received timely messages under the chosen policy.

Build a recording sender that stores a submission but returns uncertainty. Process the same task again and inspect the local attempt identities and resulting state. It must not invent evidence of delivery. ¶ Add expiry and current-preference checks. Then switch provider after the uncertain attempt and show why this can produce a second external effect. Implement only the recovery behaviour your mock provider contract supports, and make any residual duplicate risk explicit in the result.''',
['Durable notification intent', 'Preference and template policy', 'Bounded channel submission', 'Verified callback or reconciliation'],
['Intent owns delivery tasks', 'Task has versioned template', 'Attempt has provider reference', 'Callback inbox deduplicates events'],
'def deliver(task, now):\n    require_current_attempt(task)\n    if now >= task.expires_at: return mark_expired(task)\n    if not permitted_now(task): return mark_cancelled(task)\n    sender = sender_factory.for_channel(task.channel)\n    result = sender.send(task.stable_provider_key, render(task))\n    if result.uncertain:\n        return persist_uncertain(task, result.reference)\n    return persist_submission_result(task, result)\n# A provider acknowledgment is not a human-read receipt.')

case(5, '''Users publish posts, follow accounts, and read a ranked home feed. The feed must support bounded continuation and obey privacy, blocking, moderation, and deletion rules. ¶ Ranking chooses among posts the viewer is allowed to see. A high score cannot override denial. Engagement counts and candidate lists can update later than the original post, but the product needs to state what freshness those derived views permit. Separate finding interesting content from deciding which content is authorised.

Assume a 300 ms p99 feed target under the supported workload. Retain accepted posts and define how quickly access changes must take effect. Pagination should have understandable duplicate and omission behaviour. ¶ Ranking can change between refreshes. Decide whether each refresh begins a new generation and what a continuation cursor preserves. Cached candidates may be old, so specify which properties are rechecked at serving time. These conditions are part of a useful feed response alongside its latency.

Assume ten million daily readers, twenty feed reads each, and one million posts/day. The read total is 200 million/day, or about 2,315/s on average. ¶ For a simplified ordinary-author model, assume one hundred followers per author and 32 bytes per materialised candidate reference. Fan-out creates one hundred million references/day, or 3.2 GB raw. A celebrity with millions of followers is a different traffic class. The average cannot describe that author's burst or inactive followers' unused work.

Begin with indexed posts and follow relationships, building the feed when requested. This avoids writing a copy for every follower and keeps the initial system small. ¶ As users follow more authors, candidate retrieval and ranking can require too much work per read. Measure those costs and the follower distribution. Materialising every feed immediately can replace read pressure with large unused write pressure. Introduce that trade-off only when the repeated read work justifies maintaining another representation.

POST /posts creates a versioned post; POST /follows changes the relationship. GET /feed returns a bounded page, continuation cursor, and relevant ranking generation. DELETE /posts/{id} records authoritative removal. ¶ Bind cursors to the viewer and applicable filters, and explain their snapshot or continuation semantics. A cursor does not grant continuing permission to content after access changes. Validate it and reapply the required visibility policy whenever the next page is served.

Post records include identity, author, version, visibility, state, and creation time. Follow records connect follower and author. Optional feed candidates connect a reader to a post and source version with ranking information. ¶ Keep model version or generation where reproducibility needs it. Candidates are derived references, while posts and access relationships remain authoritative. Stable source identity lets workers recognise repeated work, remove or suppress old results, and rebuild views. State whether copied features are snapshots or values expected to follow updates.

PostService owns publication and FollowService owns relationship changes. CandidateGenerator retrieves or prepares references. FeatureFetcher supplies bounded ranking inputs, Ranker scores them, and VisibilityFilter enforces access. FeedAssembler combines results, removes duplicates, and produces pages. ¶ Keep the access decision independent of desirability. Missing features can change order; they must not broaden visibility. These roles let performance work focus on candidate or feature costs while preserving a separate rule for what the viewer may receive.

Use a durable publication path with an outbox. Workers prepare candidates for ordinary authors; high-fan-out authors can be pulled at read time. Candidate storage, ranking compute, and permitted caches complete the serving path. ¶ This hybrid avoids immediately writing to every celebrity follower while making common reads cheaper. It adds strategy-selection and reconciliation work. Separate model deployment decisions from the live data path, and preserve source state and access checks when cached or materialised results lag.

Publication commits the post and fan-out intent. Workers add candidate references under stable identity. A read combines prepared and pulled candidates, checks current required permissions and post state, obtains bounded features, ranks, deduplicates, and returns a continuation. ¶ Track the generation involved. A deleted candidate may physically remain while serving correctly rejects it. That is acceptable only under the chosen deletion policy. Storage cleanup and permission to expose the reference are separate events with different completion requirements.

The assumed 200 million daily reads average about 2,315/s. With an illustrative fivefold peak, the rate becomes about 11,574/s. One million posts times one hundred followers produces one hundred million candidate writes/day. ¶ A single post by an author with ten million followers creates a separate burst. Also estimate candidates ranked per read and feature-lookup amplification. API rates alone miss much of the storage and compute work, especially when an apparently small feed request fans out to many dependencies.

Assume 20 ms at the edge, 50 ms retrieving candidates, 80 ms fetching features, 70 ms ranking, and 30 ms preparing the response. The proposed path sums to 250 ms before additional tail and queue allowance. ¶ Measure against the 300 ms objective rather than calling this sum p99. A required slow feature call can dominate the request. Define a safe missing-feature response, and measure publication freshness separately from read latency. Fast reads can still show an outdated or empty feed.

A worker can repeat fan-out after a crash, so candidate identity must suppress duplicate effects. A celebrity burst needs the selected pull or controlled distribution path rather than unlimited queue growth. ¶ Candidate cleanup can lag privacy changes, making current serving checks important. If ranking fails, an authorised chronological feed may be an acceptable fallback. It must still reject deleted or forbidden content. Test the fallback's semantics as well as its speed; returning something quickly does not make it a valid feed.

Partition candidate work by a measured ownership key, batch feature reads, and cache values within their freshness scope. Bound how many candidates a read retrieves and ranks. Give rebuilds separate budgets so they do not consume all live-serving capacity. ¶ Do not increase workers past storage capacity. Choose the hybrid threshold from observed write amplification, reader activity, and read cost. Moving an author between push and pull needs a transition that prevents missing or duplicated candidates. A threshold is a design parameter to revisit, not a universal follower count.

Post identity, updates, and deletion need a suitable local transactional authority. Feed projections and engagement totals may propagate later. An author expecting to see their own new post may need a direct-source overlay or another supported path. ¶ Access policy must still meet its own freshness rule when candidates are stale. Specify which parts of a page belong to a ranking snapshot and which are revalidated. The feed has several consistency boundaries, so one label cannot describe all its accepted observations.

Enforce author permissions, viewer visibility, blocks, and moderation on each relevant path. Validate cursors, bound scraping and feature-query amplification, and keep client-provided scores from influencing permission. ¶ Ranking logs can contain private behaviour, so retain only justified information with controlled access. Propagate required deletion into feature and analytical copies as well as post storage. The model's input and output paths need the same tenant and content-scope discipline as the main feed response.

Version rankers and use representative canary cohorts, checking rollback compatibility with stored features. Roll fan-out formats out so mixed-version readers remain valid. ¶ Keep rebuilding capacity separate from live publication and reads. A region-local feed needs a defined route for source and access-policy changes before it can promise both locality and the required privacy behaviour. Model release is another kind of serving change, so observe it with the same care as application code and schema deployment.

Track publication-to-candidate delay, candidate counts, denied visibility, read latency, feature tails, model version, duplication, and hot-author backlog. Measure relevance and useful content separately from infrastructure timing. ¶ A low p99 does not establish that pages contain correct, varied, current results. Pair operational indicators with suitable content and privacy checks. Detailed evidence should identify generations and source versions without making every post or user an unbounded metric label.

Costs include candidate-write amplification, feature storage and reads, ranking CPU, responses, and rebuilds. The simplified 3.2 GB/day of raw references excludes indexes and other metadata. ¶ Compare push and pull using how many followers actually read. Preparing feeds for inactive users can spend work that is never used. Use explicit fictional rates or current verified quotes. A cost comparison is meaningful only when visibility, retention, freshness, and recovery requirements are equal.

Fan-out-on-write spends more when posts arrive to make reads cheaper. Fan-out-on-read avoids unused preparation but adds work to each read. A hybrid targets different traffic classes while adding policy and transition complexity. ¶ Precomputed ranking can reduce serving CPU and introduce another freshness delay. Compare these choices against actual active-reader ratios and author skew. A lower average read time obtained by weakening deletion or privacy is a different contract. Keep that distinction visible when evaluating the trade-off.

Start with indexed pull reads, then add bounded ranking and useful caching. Materialise ordinary-author candidates when repeated read work becomes the limiting cost. Introduce a separate high-fan-out path after observing skew. ¶ Regional copies require source-version and privacy-update rules. Revisit thresholds as posting, reading, and follow distributions change. Stable candidate identity and explicit generation boundaries should survive each stage so the new arrangement preserves continuation and duplicate behaviour.

Test the assumptions about inactive readers, high-degree authors, and revoked access. Place a post in cached candidate pages, then delete it or make it private. Verify that later serving follows the required rule. ¶ Replay a fan-out event, pause a worker, and disable a feature dependency. Check both the resulting feed and fallback access. A drained queue does not prove that every intended reader received one usable authorised candidate; validate the identities and source versions involved.

Build push and pull candidate generation over a small follow graph with one very high-degree author. Count writes and reads for explicit user journeys rather than relying only on averages. ¶ After materialising candidates, delete a post and check the serving filter. Replay a fan-out event and verify there is no visible duplicate. Explain which strategy spends work for inactive readers and which moves it into active reads, keeping privacy checks identical in both.''',
['Post and outbox commit', 'Push ordinary; pull hot authors', 'Merge authorized candidates', 'Rank, paginate and revalidate'],
['Author owns many Posts', 'Follow maps reader to author', 'Candidate references source version', 'Generation identifies ranked page'],
'def feed(reader, cursor):\n    candidates = materialized(reader) + pull_hot_authors(reader)\n    unique = deduplicate_by_post_id(candidates)\n    permitted = [p for p in unique if visible_now(reader, p)]\n    features = fetch_bounded_features(permitted)\n    ranked = rank(permitted, features, model_version)\n    return page(ranked, validated_cursor(cursor, reader))')

case(6, '''An owner uploads a video, the service validates and converts it, and viewers receive an authorised playback manifest and media segments. Support resumable uploads, processing progress, and deletion. ¶ Adaptive playback chooses among available renditions as conditions change. It cannot correct a missing segment or a manifest published before its required outputs exist. The product therefore needs both a byte-transformation workflow and a clear decision that a particular generation is ready to play.

Preserve original and required derived media according to stated durability and retention rules. Set playback-start and smoothness objectives for supported clients, measured through the delivery journey. ¶ Asynchronous processing must expose failed and review states, not leave users waiting on an unexplained spinner. Protect private manifests, segments, thumbnails, and download links as well as the player page. A private metadata row cannot compensate for public media objects that anyone can fetch directly.

Assume 1,000 uploads/day, each averaging 1 GB and thirty minutes. Raw input is 1 TB/day and 500 source-hours/day. Assume the chosen renditions produce five output GB for each source GB, giving 5 TB/day derived data. ¶ For 10,000 simultaneous viewers averaging 3 Mb/s each, delivery is 30 Gb/s, or 3.75 GB/s, before overhead. Encoding, retention, and playback therefore impose different capacity needs. These ratios are teaching assumptions rather than codec or infrastructure benchmarks.

A prototype can accept an upload and have one worker produce one playback file. It is simple but places large transfer and computation behind fragile local execution and offers little adaptation to changing networks. ¶ Direct upload to storage removes much of the byte traffic from application workers. Durable encoding tasks preserve required progress. Begin with a small supported rendition set. Producing every possible format immediately adds compute and storage that may never be useful to the intended viewers.

POST /videos creates an upload intent. Scoped multipart authorisation permits the byte transfer. A completion operation validates the stored content, a status endpoint reports processing, and a playback endpoint returns an authorised manifest reference. ¶ Deletion tracks the required cleanup. Define the scope, lifetime, and cache behaviour of playback capabilities. An old page URL should not silently grant indefinite access. The status contract must distinguish upload complete, processing, playable, and failed rather than treating every accepted byte as ready media.

Store video identity, owner, source version, and state. Upload sessions track unfinished transfers. Encoding jobs identify input digest, profile version, and attempt epoch. Renditions identify profile, version, object key, and checksum; a manifest generation names its required artifacts. ¶ Publish only when that set is verified. Do not change bytes behind an immutable published reference without a version transition. Link derived objects to their source and owner so deletion, repair, and inventory reconciliation can find the whole media lifecycle.

UploadService owns upload permission. MediaValidator checks supported bounded input. EncodingPlanner chooses profiles, WorkerLease manages attempts, and TranscoderAdapter performs isolated conversion. ArtifactPublisher advances published metadata when the required outputs are verified. ¶ PlaybackService checks access and issues scoped delivery references. Keep transformation separate from publication. A worker can finish writing one file without making the video's complete generation playable. The metadata transition needs evidence about the entire required artifact set.

Use object storage for immutable sources and segments, a durable workflow and task queue, isolated transcoders, metadata storage, and an edge delivery path. The application handles control and access while large bytes flow directly through storage and CDN facilities. ¶ Keep source and derived retention policies explicit. Untrusted media parsers need limited resources and suitable execution and egress permissions. The source store, workflow state, and published generation each have recovery responsibilities that a task queue alone does not supply.

Validate completed upload identity, choose a versioned encoding plan, and produce artifacts under an attempt identity. Verify the required segment and manifest set, then atomically publish the playable metadata generation. ¶ Failed attempts leave unpublished outputs for cleanup or safe reuse. A client obtains an authorised manifest and fetches supported renditions through the delivery path. Marking ready after the first output is valid only if progressive publication is an explicit supported contract. Otherwise it exposes a partially usable video as complete.

Thirty days under the assumptions need 30 TB of original data plus 150 TB of renditions before extra copies and overhead. Viewer delivery at 30 Gb/s is far larger than the metadata API's transfer. ¶ Encoding cost needs measured source-duration and codec ratios. If an assumed two compute-hours are needed per source-hour, 500 source-hours/day require 1,000 compute-hours/day. This is a model input, not a performance result. Use real content and profiles to measure the ratio before sizing workers.

Assume 50 ms for playback authorisation, 30 ms for manifest delivery, and 800 ms for initial buffering. That path totals 880 ms in the example. ¶ A cache miss or a constrained client changes it. Measure startup, rebuffering, rendition switches, and failures independently of the metadata API's p99. The viewer's experience includes segment delivery and player behaviour. A fast permission endpoint establishes little about a missing or slowly delivered first segment.

An upload stops halfway. An input is corrupt. A worker writes two segments and crashes before the third. A manifest points to a deleted object. Each needs a distinct state and supported repair path. ¶ Keep unfinished generations unpublished and validate the final set. Protect the origin if the edge path fails; fallback can suddenly send enormous video traffic to it. Deletion and revoked access must affect playable references and copies according to the chosen capability and cache policy. Test through the actual media path.

Scale upload bytes, encoding jobs, and viewer delivery separately. Bound worker concurrency and prioritise encoding according to deadlines. Immutable segments are suitable for caching where access policy permits it. ¶ A viral video creates hot-object delivery pressure, not necessarily a metadata-sharding problem. Plan origin protection and regional copy cost during cache loss. Adding profiles or transcoders can also increase storage and backfill load. Improve the measured limiting path rather than expanding every component together.

The published metadata generation must name a usable artifact set. Encoding attempts can repeat using stable output identities and conditional completion, so an old worker cannot replace a newer generation. ¶ Playback caches have a declared reuse interval; private access and deletion have their own requirements. Before using regional copies for recovery, ensure the publication protocol establishes the required objects there. Merely copying some bytes does not make a region an authoritative complete failover source.

Authorise upload and playback, limit size and supported formats, and isolate parsers and encoders. Give workers narrowly scoped storage access. Protect signed capabilities and keep them out of routine logs. ¶ Check manifest, segment, and thumbnail delivery: private metadata can coexist accidentally with public objects. Test capability expiry, cache keys, membership revocation, and deletion end to end. The player's page is only one route to the content and should not be the only place enforcing its privacy.

Version encoding profiles and retain usable older outputs until a validated transition permits removal. Workers need durable claims and graceful shutdown. Expensive backfills should not consume playback or live-processing capacity. ¶ Test recovery when source bytes survive but metadata is restored to an older point. Artifact inventories and source identities must let the application reconcile what exists. A rollout that preserves running containers but loses the link between metadata and media can still leave acknowledged videos unusable.

Observe upload completion, oldest encoding jobs, failed profiles, compute-to-media ratios, and ready-generation integrity. For playback, track edge hits, origin transfer, startup delay, rebuffering, and access outcomes. ¶ Correlate source, profile, and playback generation in appropriate detail. Alert on missing segments and videos stuck processing, even if workers report few exceptions. A synthetic private-video journey should include permitted playback and later revocation, so monitoring covers content access as well as infrastructure availability.

Include originals, renditions, repeated encoding, compute, delivery requests, transfer, metadata, and telemetry. Sustaining the assumed 3.75 GB/s for an hour delivers 13.5 TB before overhead. ¶ The duration of viewer demand matters as much as a peak concurrency label. Compare profile count and retention with quality needs. Use fictional labelled rates for examples or verified current quotes for spending. A smaller rendition set can save costs while changing playback choices, so evaluate the user effect as well as the storage total.

A single file is easy to manage but adapts poorly to varying delivery conditions. Segmented adaptive delivery adds manifests and a larger artifact lifecycle. Pre-encoding offers readiness while consuming work and storage for profiles that may never be used. ¶ On-demand encoding can reduce unused work and create startup or burst risk. Managed conversion moves selected operations to a provider while leaving publication, access, and reconciliation decisions. Compare alternatives with the same supported-client, privacy, and recovery requirements.

Start with resumable upload and durable processing identity. Add a small tested adaptive set and versioned publication. Then introduce suitable edge delivery and scale encoding separately from playback. ¶ Add regional objects or more profiles for demonstrated locality, recovery, or quality needs. Keep exact source and profile identities so every result remains explainable and reproducible. More delivery capacity should not change what ready, private, or deleted means for an existing video.

Ask whether every ready generation is actually playable, whether its private segments remain protected, and whether origin survives edge loss. These are separate claims requiring separate evidence. ¶ Test corrupt input, worker interruption between artifacts and publication, repeated completion, a missing segment, expired access, and deletion after caching. A video that played once through a warm public URL proves little about private recovery, partial encoding, or the failed-delivery path.

Build a toy publisher requiring three segments. Write two and stop. The manifest must remain unpublished. Resume using the same job identity, write the third, validate checksums, and advance one metadata generation atomically. ¶ Then remove a segment deliberately and run an integrity check. The check should identify the broken published set. Explain which boundary establishes ready state and which later failure the integrity check detects, rather than using one success flag for both.''',
['Resumable source upload', 'Versioned isolated encoding jobs', 'Validate and publish generation', 'Authorized adaptive edge delivery'],
['Video owns source version', 'Job references profile and epoch', 'Rendition has immutable artifacts', 'Manifest generation references complete set'],
'def publish(job):\n    require_current_epoch(job)\n    artifacts = expected_artifacts(job.source, job.profile)\n    require(all(exists_and_valid(a) for a in artifacts))\n    with transaction():\n        assert_current_epoch(job)\n        generation = record_complete_manifest(artifacts)\n        mark_playable(job.video, generation)\n# Partial worker output is never silently declared ready.')

case(7, '''A driver reports location and availability. A rider requests a trip; the system finds candidates, offers work, and records one accepted assignment. It also tracks trip progress and cancellation. ¶ Being nearby makes a driver a candidate, not a confirmed owner of the trip. Define offer expiry and the rider's view while matching is unresolved. The same driver must not become actively assigned to incompatible trips merely because two matchers saw an old available state.

Set a bounded machine-side dispatch target for the assumed region and preserve exclusive active assignments. Protect precise location information. Treat the freshness and accuracy of reported positions as uncertainty, not ground truth. ¶ Trip transitions must survive retries and temporary disconnection. A missing heartbeat does not establish that a trip ended. Human offer acceptance and provider payment status also have different timelines. Keep these states visible rather than collapsing every delay into one failed trip outcome.

Assume 100,000 online drivers report every five seconds. The rate is 20,000 updates/s. At 100 raw bytes per position, that is 2 MB/s, or 172.8 GB/day if every update is retained. ¶ Assume 100 new trip requests/s. Map calls, candidate evaluation, regional skew, and location retention require separate estimates. The large location stream and the smaller authoritative assignment workload do not need the same storage or coordination mechanism.

Begin with one service that stores recent positions and selects a nearby available driver. It is straightforward while requests are handled sequentially, but concurrent matchers can select the same driver. ¶ Separate the location projection used for search from authoritative assignment state. Preserve exclusive claims before optimising spatial queries. A better nearest-neighbour lookup improves candidates; it cannot resolve two buyers of the same driver's availability. Stale positions can also reduce match quality even when the assignment invariant remains correct.

A driver location update carries position, source sequence, and observed time. POST /trips creates a stable rider request. POST /offers/{id}/accept attempts a valid claim, while trip status and cancellation expose explicit transitions. ¶ Derive driver identity from authentication and validate plausible input. An expired or superseded offer returns a defined conflict. A repeated accept can recover the existing result. The API must not permit a caller-supplied driver identifier to replace another assignment without passing the ownership rule.

Keep driver_state with availability, assignment_id, and version. Trip records contain rider, state, driver, and version. Offers identify the trip, driver, expiry, and status. Latest positions carry driver sequence, spatial cell, and observation time. ¶ Retained location history has a separate restricted lifecycle. A conditional transition or appropriate uniqueness constraint protects active assignment. The spatial index is derived candidate state; it must not become a second independent authority for whether a driver is free.

LocationIngestor validates updates and their order. SpatialIndex finds candidates, Matcher ranks a bounded set, and OfferService manages offer validity. AssignmentRepository performs the authoritative claim, while TripStateMachine controls later transitions. ¶ Map and ETA adapters have their own deadlines. Their failure must not corrupt assignment. Stable trip and acceptance identities let clients retry without starting different logical work. Keep the availability decision at its atomic owner even when candidate selection runs on many workers.

Use a regional location stream and spatial projection, a durable trip/assignment store, matching workers, offer delivery, and trip events. Partition geography for useful query locality while defining driver movement across boundaries. ¶ Configuration can come from a control plane, but stale independent availability copies must not each accept the same driver. The placement needs one valid authority or transfer protocol for assignment. Region-local candidate reads and exclusive ownership are different paths and should appear separately in the design.

Commit one rider request identity. Search for sufficiently fresh candidates, recheck authoritative availability, and create a bounded-lifetime offer. Acceptance verifies that the offer and ownership conditions still hold. ¶ Atomically bind the driver and trip under the chosen transaction model. Replay repeated accepts or reject stale ones. If the reply is lost, trip status resolves the client's uncertainty. Receiving an offer is not itself the assignment decision; the conditional acceptance is the point that prevents incompatible winners.

The example ingests 20,000 locations/s and receives 100 dispatches/s. If each dispatch evaluates twenty candidates, that adds 2,000 candidate evaluations/s before retries or ETA calls. ¶ Do not call a route service for every location update without justification. Busy centres and major events can concentrate work in one area while global averages look mild. Estimate regional peaks, hot cells, and movement across their edges. Candidate pruning can reduce map cost without changing the exclusive assignment rule.

Assume 30 ms ingress, 40 ms candidate lookup, 80 ms bounded ETA evaluation, and 30 ms assignment work. The machine-side planning budget sums to 180 ms. ¶ Human acceptance is a separate interval. Missing candidates, offer expiry, communication delays, and retries can extend the matching journey. Measure these states rather than announcing one optimistic dispatch latency. The budget is illustrative and needs validation for actual city shapes, traffic, and provider behaviour.

Reject late location updates that would move the serving projection backward under its sequence rule. When two requests select one driver, only one authoritative claim may succeed. A paused worker cannot accept a superseded offer after it resumes. ¶ Lost offers or replies need status recovery. A map outage may permit a justified simpler ranking or require pausing matching. It must not bypass exclusive assignment. Test each failure at the relevant state boundary so a candidate-search problem does not become a conflicting trip commitment.

Scale the location stream and spatial serving independently from assignment transactions. Batch or prune ETA calls. Partition regions or cells with an explicit handoff that preserves the current driver's authority. ¶ Candidate caches need freshness information. More matchers do not divide a hot serialised claim owner automatically; changing that model must still preserve one active assignment. Observe the actual concentrated work before choosing finer partitions, since they also increase cross-cell queries and movement coordination.

Location can update through a delayed projection with an explicit age policy. Driver and trip assignment require a coordinated conditional transition. Crossing a regional boundary needs authority transfer rather than independent acceptance in both regions. ¶ Give trip status the required read-after-accept behaviour. Time helps judge whether a position is recent, but sequence and fencing rules handle stale updates and owners. These different forms of state should not all be given the same broad consistency label.

Authenticate driver updates and restrict rider and driver views to the relevant trip context. Limit access to precise location history, including support and administrative paths. Retain only justified history and audit privileged use. ¶ Bound geographic scraping and expensive map requests. A rider cannot select an arbitrary driver identity as authority to claim them. Keep raw coordinates out of broad telemetry where unnecessary. Privacy follows locations through streams, candidate caches, exports, and retained logs, not just the map displayed to the user.

Use regional placement with a stated failure model and durable assignment state. Roll releases compatibly with old clients and offers already in progress. Drain new work while preserving existing offer and trip records. ¶ Disaster recovery must reconcile active assignments and uncertain provider outcomes. Restoring an old snapshot must not reoffer drivers still assigned elsewhere. The replacement needs valid history and fencing before it acts, even if the API has resumed responding. Test the recovery path with active trips rather than only an empty system.

Track location age, stale-update rejection, cell skew, candidate counts, claim conflicts, expiry, matching outcomes, and time spent in each dispatch state. Observe map-provider errors separately. ¶ Correlate stable creation and acceptance identities across attempts. Alert on unresolved assignments and invariant violations, not only HTTP errors. Simulated drivers can move across cells, disconnect, and accept near expiry while concurrent riders compete. These journeys test the distinction between fresh candidates and correct exclusive outcomes.

Include ingestion, hot location state, maps/ETA calls, assignments, offer attempts, history retention, transfer, and operations. A route lookup for every position update could dominate the cost despite efficient state storage. ¶ Model calls per candidate and dispatch, then compare pruning and batching. Use explicit fictional rates or verified commercial terms. The example does not claim a real company's internal bill. Keep the accuracy and latency effects of a cheaper candidate strategy visible alongside its savings.

Straight-line proximity is cheap but may ignore road travel. Route-based ETA can improve ranking while adding delay and an external dependency. Sequential offers reduce simultaneous acceptance opportunities; multi-offer designs need a controlled single winner. ¶ Fine spatial cells improve some lookup locality but add boundary queries and handoff work. Compare fairness and rider outcomes too. Candidate quality, human wait, and machine cost all matter, while none can justify weakening the active-assignment invariant.

First establish a correct regional claim and basic proximity search. Add streamed positions and spatial indexing when lookup work requires it. Then distribute matching with bounded ETA evaluation. ¶ Split geographic ownership after measuring hot areas, using a safe transfer procedure. Regional serving adds another partition and failure policy for active trips. Preserve stable identities and claims as the deployment grows. Replicating an apparently stateless matcher does not replicate the assignment authority it depends on.

Review the meanings of nearby, recently observed, available, offered, and assigned. The spatial index answers only some of those questions. ¶ Test two riders choosing one driver, out-of-order positions, acceptance after expiry, paused workers, and ownership handoff. One claim should win and losers should receive a meaningful state. Confirm the search projection never acts as an independent writer of authoritative availability. A fast matching demo can otherwise hide the most consequential race.

Build an optimistic driver claim with expected version and offer expiry. Start two requests using the same initial version. Verify one claim succeeds locally and one returns conflict. ¶ Then deliver an older location and a delayed acceptance after the winner commits. Neither should overwrite the active assignment. Explain the actual protected boundary: the local transaction can establish one local owner, while external trip or payment effects still need their own contracts and recovery evidence.''',
['Ordered driver position ingestion', 'Fresh candidate search', 'Conditional exclusive driver claim', 'Durable trip state and recovery'],
['Driver has one active assignment', 'Trip owns lifecycle state', 'Offer has expiry and version', 'Location is a versioned projection'],
'def accept_offer(offer, driver, now):\n    with transaction():\n        current = lock_offer(offer.id)\n        require(current.pending and now < current.expires_at)\n        won = claim_driver_if_available(driver, current.expected_version)\n        if not won: raise AssignmentConflict\n        bind_trip_and_offer(offer.trip, driver, offer.id)\n    return current_trip_state(offer.trip)')

case(8, '''A buyer browses an event, holds selected seats, pays, and receives tickets. Holds last for a defined period; cancellation and refunds follow an explicit policy. ¶ The displayed seat map is advisory. The authoritative reservation decides who owns the opportunity to buy a seat. Retries, expiry tasks, and payment callbacks may overlap, but must not confirm the same seat for two buyers. Design that invariant before optimising the map or launching a waiting room.

Prevent duplicate confirmed ownership and retain accepted payment and ticket records under the chosen failure contract. Pending reservations must resolve through supported confirmation, expiry, or recovery. ¶ Set booking and waiting-room objectives for launches, including the intended fairness policy. Fixed inventory cannot satisfy every simultaneous request. Reject or queue excess demand clearly instead of promising universal acceptance and overselling. Separate an admitted transaction's latency from the user's total time waiting for admission and payment.

Assume 50,000 seats and a short peak of 100,000 reservation attempts/s. At 200 raw bytes per seat, seat records occupy about 10 MB before indexes. ¶ The small byte total does not make the launch easy. Many callers compete for the same limited ownership transitions. Assume two-minute holds and a bounded number of seats per user. Retry volume, transaction conflicts, and expiry work need their own capacity estimates. These assumptions are a launch scenario, not a benchmark for a particular database.

Start with a transactional seat table. Claim a seat using a conditional change from available to reserved, and insert its reservation in the same transaction. ¶ A read followed by an unconditional update can let both callers report success. The conditional claim must check its result. This first correct boundary handles simpler traffic; flash demand, payment uncertainty, and expiry races motivate further controls. Fixing concurrency first prevents a faster interface from merely producing incorrect tickets more quickly.

POST /reservations includes event, seat identities, and an idempotency key. The returned resource exposes status and expiry. A payment operation belongs to that reservation, and authorised ticket reads show committed issuance. ¶ Repeat keys recover the established result rather than claiming another seat. Bind them to the trusted actor and request content. Payment states distinguish authorisation, capture, decline, and uncertainty according to the provider. An expired reservation and an uncertain payment are different conditions needing different next actions.

A seat identifies event and seat, state, reservation owner, and version. Reservation records hold owner, state, expiry, and version, with a relationship to selected seats. Payment operations and ticket records have their own identities. ¶ Enforce one relevant confirmed active ticket per seat. Expiry and confirmation check the reservation identity and current state/version. An old expiry task must not release a seat now held by a later reservation. These relationships need actual constraints or atomic checks, not just matching names in a diagram.

SeatRepository performs conditional claims or locks. ReservationService manages selection and hold rules. PaymentWorkflow handles external effects, TicketIssuer commits valid issuance, and ExpiryWorker conditionally releases old holds. ¶ Pricing and refund rules use explicit amounts and units. Enforce ownership at the shared state boundary so callbacks and workers cannot bypass a check implemented only in an HTTP handler. Each role should return enough state to distinguish conflict, expiry, confirmed success, and an uncertain provider result.

Place bounded admission or a waiting room before the ownership store. Keep durable reservation state, the payment adapter, a workflow or outbox path, and a ticket read model. Cache descriptions and advisory maps separately. ¶ Event partitioning can keep one event's seat operations local, but a popular event remains hot. Admission and contention control still matter. The cache answers what may be available; the transaction answers what this buyer actually reserved. Do not let those paths become competing allocation authorities.

Atomically claim seats and create the hold, then return its expiry after commit. Submit payment with stable provider identity. Confirmation verifies a still-valid transition and commits the required reservation, seat, and ticket changes. ¶ If payment succeeds after expiry, follow a defined recovery policy: reacquire only if safe or refund according to the contract. Never issue against a seat already owned by someone else. Expiry checks current identity and version before release, so delayed tasks cannot undo a later valid outcome.

Assume admission is configured to allow 2,000 reservation attempts/s out of a 100,000/s launch. The remaining callers wait or receive rejection under the stated policy. That 2,000 limit is an input to validate, not demonstrated capacity. ¶ A two-minute hold is bounded by the number of seats, but the surrounding retry traffic is not. Estimate writes per attempt, repeated-key checks, conflicts, and expiry scans. Web concurrency can grow rapidly while the small inventory remains the same.

Assume 20 ms edge time, 30 ms acquiring the transaction resources, 40 ms for conditional seat/reservation work, and 20 ms returning the result. The admitted path totals 110 ms in this scenario. ¶ Contention can exceed that budget, so bound waiting and stop work that is no longer useful. Report waiting-room time separately. Payment has its own expiry-sensitive timeline. Fast admitted requests do not establish a fast journey for users still queued outside the allocation boundary.

Two buyers must not both win after reading available. A crash after reservation commit but before reply needs repeat-key recovery. Duplicate payment evidence must not issue another ticket. ¶ Also test payment after hold expiry and stale expiry after confirmation or reassignment. Conditional current-state transitions and reconciliation protect these overlaps. If email fails after a ticket commits, the ticket still exists and needs another delivery path. Communication failure must not be mistaken for failure of the already accepted ownership transition.

Cache advisory data, bound launch admission, and shorten the correct seat transaction. Partition independent events when measured load justifies it. ¶ Multi-seat claims need a deliberate all-or-nothing rule and consistent lock order. Splitting an event across shards can make group bookings harder. Extra shards do not split contention over one particular seat. Scale the work around the invariant while preserving its atomic owner, and introduce a more complex allocation protocol only when its benefit exceeds the added transaction and recovery burden.

Seat ownership and ticket issuance need an atomic authoritative rule. Availability displays may lag if that is clear to the buyer. Payment and email are external effects with stable identities and tracked workflow state. ¶ A serializable local transaction can help with local concurrency but does not include the provider automatically. Handle retryable serialization failures with bounded whole-transaction retries and safe external-effect placement. State which records move together and which later outcomes are reconciled, rather than claiming the entire booking journey is one database transaction.

Authorise holds, payments, tickets, transfers, and refunds. Calculate prices on the server, verify provider callbacks, and protect credentials. Limit automated and bulk abuse under a defined identity policy. ¶ Scope idempotency keys so one actor cannot recover another's result. If tickets use download capabilities, set their scope and lifetime and audit important changes. A waiting room protects capacity; it is not proof of authorised identity or fairness. Those policies still need their own enforcement and tests.

Keep reservation states and expiry meanings compatible during rollout. Durable scheduled expiry survives worker replacement. Database promotion must respect the acknowledged-data policy. ¶ After recovery, reconcile provider results before declaring all bookings resolved. When write authority is uncertain, temporarily pausing allocation can protect exclusive ownership while descriptions remain readable. Test these transitions with live holds and callbacks, not only an empty event. A newly running server must not treat an old snapshot as permission to sell seats already confirmed elsewhere.

Track admitted attempts, claim conflicts, transaction waits, hold age, expiry backlog, payment uncertainty, late success, and ticket uniqueness. Follow one logical booking through repeated requests and callbacks. ¶ Run appropriate invariant checks over seats, reservations, and tickets, with reconciliation against payment evidence. Empty error logs cannot establish that the relationships agree. Keep user-visible waiting and completion times separate from raw API times so operational dashboards reflect the actual booking journey.

Include burst admission, transactional work, abandoned holds, payment calls, delivery, retention, telemetry, and recovery capacity. Model the duration of a launch rather than charging its peak rate for every hour of the month. ¶ More web workers cannot remove contention on one seat. Compare alternatives using the same no-double-ticket and recovery requirements. Use explicit fictional rates or verified prices, and include retry and expiry work that occurs even for buyers who never complete a purchase.

Pessimistic locking serialises access early but can create waiting and deadlocks. Conditional optimistic claims return conflicts quickly but require clear retry and user handling. One event owner can simplify order while imposing a capacity boundary. ¶ Distributed allocation can reduce some central work and complicate multi-seat atomicity and fairness. Choose by the actual seat and hold contract. The mechanism must explain one winner under concurrent attempts, then integrate that winner with payment and expiry rather than stopping at a successful lock acquisition.

First implement the seat transaction and repeat-key result. Add durable expiry and provider reconciliation. Then protect launches with admission and advisory caching. ¶ Partition independent events after measuring the workload. Change one-event allocation only when its demonstrated constraints justify the added protocol. Recheck no-double-ticket behaviour at every stage. Increasing traffic capacity is not progress if an earlier safe expiry or retry becomes an uncoordinated write in the new arrangement.

Check that expiry and confirmation refer to the same reservation identity and current state. Review the recovery for a successful payment after the hold is gone, and confirm that display state never grants ownership. ¶ Test competing buyers, repeated callbacks, commit-then-crash, stale expiry after reassignment, and late payment. A correct happy-path seat update answers only part of the workflow. The review must also account for money and tickets that remain uncertain after the visible request ends.

Reproduce the last-seat race using two connections or an explicit interleaving. Show both buyers reporting success under naive reads and writes. Replace it with a conditional claim and reservation transaction, checking affected rows. ¶ Add versioned expiry. Confirm or reassign the seat, then deliver the old expiry task. It must leave the current owner's seat unchanged. Explain the condition that prevents the stale release, rather than relying on the test's usual timing.''',
['Admit bounded booking attempt', 'Atomic seat reservation and expiry', 'Stable payment operation', 'Conditional ticket issuance or compensation'],
['Event owns seats', 'Reservation owns held seat set', 'Payment operation has stable identity', 'One ticket per event-seat invariant'],
'with transaction():\n    insert_reservation(reservation_id, owner, expires_at)\n    updated = reserve_if_available(event, seat, reservation_id)\n    if updated != 1: raise SeatUnavailable\n# Expiry later releases only the same current reservation version.\n# Provider payment is coordinated outside this local transaction.')

case(9, '''A client creates a payment intent, the service requests authorisation or capture, and later reports the supported outcome. It records balanced accounting entries, reconciles provider evidence, and tracks refunds as separate operations. ¶ An order's status, a provider's operation, and the ledger are related but different records. Represent money with currency and integer minor units plus an explicit rounding policy. A single paid flag cannot explain the sequence, uncertainty, or accounting consequences of moving money.

Preserve financial invariants and auditable state. Prevent duplicate relevant provider effects where its contract supports that guarantee, and resolve uncertain outcomes rather than inventing success or decline. ¶ Set intent-acceptance timing separately from completion. Each settled amount should have unique balanced postings and authorised corrections under the chosen accounting model. A provider outage may leave work pending. Safe pending state is preferable to a fast unsupported final result that the system cannot later explain.

Assume one million intents/day, about 11.6/s on average, with an illustrative peak of 200/s. Suppose each initially produces six 1 kB operational/audit records: raw growth is 6 GB/day. ¶ Ledger details, indexes, copies, callbacks, and retention add to this. The model uses synthetic amounts and does not assert actual provider prices or institutional architecture. Estimate provider concurrency and reconciliation traffic separately from the daily average, especially under long waits and retries.

A first service calls the provider, sets an order flag, and replies. If the provider charges and the process dies before saving the result, a retry can repeat the charge. ¶ Before scaling, add a durable operation identity and explicit states including uncertainty. Local transactions protect related local changes. Provider keys, status queries, and reconciliation address the external gap. They solve different parts of the history. A larger deployment cannot repair an ambiguous operation for which no stable identity was recorded.

POST /payment-intents accepts amount_minor, currency, business reference, and idempotency key. The status resource reports explicitly supported states such as pending, authorised, captured, declined, or uncertain. A refund endpoint creates a separately keyed refund. ¶ Bind keys to actor and financial payload; changed content under the same key conflicts. Verify provider webhooks rather than trusting client approval fields. Define how clients wait or query after uncertainty, so a missing reply does not automatically become another financial attempt.

Keep payment_intent and operation records with actor, key, fingerprint, progress, and result. Provider attempts retain stable keys, references, and outcomes. A verified-event inbox recognises repeated evidence. ¶ The ledger has transactions and entries identifying account, currency, and signed amount. Required entries balance within the relevant currency and accounting scope. Enforce external-operation linkage and correction identity. An authorised correction creates a new accounting event instead of silently rewriting past evidence. These constraints make the relationship between provider facts and local postings reviewable.

PaymentService owns allowed transitions. Money and policy values check amounts. ProviderAdapter maps explicit outcomes, OperationRepository handles duplicate identity, and LedgerService validates and commits postings. Reconciler compares external evidence; RefundWorkflow owns refund progress. ¶ These responsibilities can begin in one application. A timeout maps to uncertainty, not a definite decline. Keep provider-specific details at the adapter while exposing enough evidence for recovery. A component boundary is useful when it preserves meaning and ownership, rather than requiring a network service for every role.

Use an authenticated API, durable intent and operation state, bounded provider workers, a verified callback inbox, a ledger boundary, and scheduled reconciliation. Where downstream updates are required, commit their publication intent with the relevant local state. ¶ Reserve capacity for reconciliation rather than letting optional analytics consume it. Regional serving must preserve the chosen ledger and provider-operation authority. Draw external calls outside the local transaction boundary so the uncertainty and recovery path remain visible.

Commit the intent and logical operation identity. Submit the stable provider key, then record either supported evidence or uncertainty. Capture or settlement evidence drives an idempotent transition creating the required balanced postings once. ¶ Callbacks may arrive before or after a synchronous reply, so transitions must not regress incorrectly. Reconciliation queries the stable reference and repairs discrepancies. It must not blindly resend a charge merely because a local status is still pending. Trace evidence and local updates in order to see where a crash can leave work unresolved.

At a stable 200 calls/s and an assumed 500 ms provider wait, Little's Law gives 200×0.5 = 100 in-flight provider calls in that boundary. The average daily rate of 11.6/s does not describe this peak. ¶ The calculation is not a safe pool recommendation: allow for variance, deadlines, provider limits, and failures. Initial operational records total 6 GB/day before other storage. Callback and reconciliation work can continue after original traffic subsides and need capacity of their own.

Assume 20 ms ingress, 20 ms committing the intent, 500 ms with the provider, and 20 ms for the final local update. The successful synchronous path totals 560 ms. ¶ These are assumed stage times, not p99 or guaranteed completion. Returning a durable pending intent after about 40 ms is a different API contract with later completion. A timeout requires status recovery or reconciliation. It supplies no reliable basis for converting an attempted payment into a definite failed business outcome.

Test a lost reply after local commit, provider execution followed by process loss, repeated or reordered callbacks, refund uncertainty, and failure to commit postings. ¶ Persist unknown outcomes and repeat only with a supported safe identity or established evidence. If the ledger is unavailable, do not bypass it by setting paid. Recovery must explain provider state and required postings before claiming the complete outcome resolved. A healthy API response after restart does not establish that the money histories now agree.

Scale independent acceptance and operation workers, while bounding provider concurrency and retaining per-payment and relevant per-account invariants. Keep reconciliation resources isolated enough to repair uncertainty during an incident. ¶ Status projections can serve reads with a stated freshness rule. Ledger partitioning needs a deliberate balance and transaction scope; one hot account can remain concentrated. A modest average daily rate alone is no reason to shard. Make the added ownership and recovery complexity answer an observed bottleneck.

Local duplicate recognition, payment transitions, and required postings need appropriate atomic boundaries. A provider is outside those local transactions and requires its own identity and recovery contract. ¶ Uniqueness and transaction semantics can protect one local posting set. They do not prove the provider executed exactly once. Refunds have separate states and may not reverse timing, fees, or downstream consequences perfectly. State what each accepted result means and preserve uncertainty until there is evidence for a final transition.

Authorise payments and refunds, validate values server-side, verify callbacks, and protect credentials. Minimise sensitive data through reviewed provider tokenisation or integration where appropriate, rather than keeping unnecessary raw credentials. ¶ Restrict and audit privileged corrections. An operator is not automatically permitted to rewrite accounting history. Bind every effect and query to its tenant and account scope. Logs should preserve relevant identity and outcomes without exposing secrets. These are architecture controls, not a claim of compliance with any particular financial or legal regime.

Roll states and event formats out compatibly. Preserve operation identities through releases and recovery, and control provider configuration and key changes. ¶ An old database restore can forget keys for effects the provider already executed. Recovery therefore needs replay protection and reconciliation before resubmission. Test delayed callbacks from an older release and return after an outage. The new process's local view must be brought into agreement with external evidence instead of treating restored pending rows as untouched work.

Separate acceptance and completion delay. Track pending/uncertain age, provider attempts, repeated-event recognition, callback verification, imbalances, unmatched settlements, refund progress, and reconciliation outcomes. ¶ Alert on invariant violations and old unresolved money movement, even when HTTP success rates are good. Evidence should distinguish attempted, provider-accepted, and locally posted states. Stable identities let an investigator reconstruct the operation without pretending that one log line or response code proves the complete financial journey.

Costs include provider attempts, secure records, ledger work, retained audit evidence, telemetry, reconciliation, and operation. Use symbolic fees and labelled hypothetical infrastructure inputs unless current prices are verified. ¶ A cheaper arrangement that cannot explain uncertain charges is a different and insufficient service. Cost per reconciled successful business payment is often more useful than cost per HTTP attempt. Retries can increase the attempt denominator precisely when useful outcomes decline. Keep the supported financial invariants equal when comparing alternatives.

Synchronous completion can suit a client expecting an immediate result but exposes provider waits and uncertainty. Asynchronous intents make pending progress explicit while adding workflow state. ¶ One local transactional application simplifies related invariants. Separating provider and ledger services can give organisational or isolation benefits and adds coordination. Append-only accounting history and mutable operational projections can complement each other. Do not force one paid record to serve every status, audit, and accounting need. Compare boundaries by the work they clarify and the recovery they require.

Begin with explicit states, currency-aware integer values, local transactions, and stable provider operation identity. Add durable callback handling and reconciliation before introducing more traffic paths. ¶ Then use projections and bounded workers for measured needs. Partition only when a demonstrated limit justifies a preserved invariant boundary. Every earlier payment must remain explainable through its provider evidence and postings after the topology changes. Scaling is successful when it expands supported work without losing that meaning.

Ask which evidence establishes financial success, who commits required postings, and how recovery handles an old deduplication snapshot beside newer provider effects. ¶ Test charge-then-crash, callback-before-reply, duplicate evidence, partial refunds, and restore from earlier local state. Check for one valid local posting set and explicit unresolved states where evidence is missing. A provider timeout must not become a convenient decline merely to make the normal request path appear finished.

Build a synthetic provider that remembers stable operation keys and a local ledger that accepts only balanced entries. Stop after provider execution but before recording the result locally. ¶ Reconcile by key and verify one logical operation leads to one balanced local posting set. Replay the provider event, and reject a changed financial payload under the old key. Explain that this demonstrates the synthetic contract and local boundary, rather than a guarantee for an arbitrary real provider.''',
['Durable payment intent and key', 'Provider effect may be uncertain', 'Verified evidence reconciles state', 'Atomic balanced ledger postings'],
['Payment owns operation identity', 'Provider attempt has external reference', 'Ledger transaction owns balanced entries', 'Refund has independent lifecycle'],
'def apply_settlement(event):\n    with transaction():\n        if inbox_contains(event.id): return prior_outcome(event.id)\n        payment = lock_payment(event.payment_id)\n        validate_transition_and_amount(payment, event)\n        entries = [debit_entry(event), credit_entry(event)]\n        require_balanced_by_currency(entries)\n        insert_unique_posting_set(event.operation_id, entries)\n        update_payment_and_insert_inbox(payment, event)\n# Provider execution is reconciled separately from this transaction.')

case(10, '''A user creates a schedule, the service identifies due occurrences, and workers execute the jobs. The user can inspect history, configure retries, and cancel supported future work. ¶ Define fixed times versus intervals, timezones, missed runs, and overlap. A schedule and one occurrence are different identities. Restarting a worker or scheduler must not invent a new logical occurrence for the same due time. Timing semantics need to be explicit before choosing a timer or queue implementation.

Keep accepted schedules and required runs recoverable under the supported failures. Bound scheduling delay and prevent stale attempts from overwriting newer results. Execution can start repeatedly around failures, so external effects need appropriate identity or fencing. ¶ Cancellation also needs a boundary: future dispatch, pending work, and best-effort interruption of a running effect are different promises. A local cancellation flag cannot prove a remote effect was undone. Make these distinctions visible in run status.

Assume one million schedules and 100,000 occurrences/hour. The average rate is 100,000/3,600 ≈ 27.8 runs/s. At an initial 2 kB of run state each, raw growth is 200 MB/hour. ¶ With ten-second mean execution at a stable rate, average active work is about 27.8×10 = 278 runs. Top-of-hour bursts, long jobs, retries, and retention add requirements. The number of saved schedules alone does not describe due-time concentration or required execution capacity.

One process scans due schedules and launches local functions. If it creates a run and advances the schedule in separate commits, a crash between them can repeat or miss the occurrence. ¶ Create a stable occurrence record and advance due state transactionally. A leader can simplify scanning, but leadership alone does not make worker effects execute once. The first improvement is durable identity and atomic progress, not simply adding a second process to repeat the same unsafe loop.

POST /schedules accepts timing, timezone, payload reference, retry rules, and overlap policy. Cancellation changes the documented future behaviour. GET /runs/{id} reports attempts and outcome. A run-once operation has its own idempotency identity. ¶ Bound timing-expression and payload complexity. Return durable acceptance separately from execution completion. Where an external effect remains uncertain, status should say so rather than treating a worker's missing reply as proof it never happened.

A schedule stores id, version, next_due, timing policy, and state. A run binds a unique (schedule_id,occurrence) to input version and progress. Attempts record epoch, lease deadline, worker, and result. ¶ Preserve original due time separately from dispatch time. Runs and attempts are not interchangeable: another attempt does not create another logical occurrence. Conditional progress prevents a superseded attempt from replacing newer state. Use outbox or effect records where durable dispatch and external identity require them.

ScheduleParser and TimePolicy calculate occurrences. DueScanner finds candidates. RunRepository inserts the run and advances schedule state atomically. Dispatcher routes work, LeaseManager grants attempts, and WorkerAdapter executes it. ¶ Reconciler finds stuck or uncertain outcomes. The scheduler owns when work is due; workers execute under a bounded current claim. Each effect adapter needs a stated repetition or fencing contract. The component split helps test time decisions separately from executing a potentially irreversible external operation.

Keep durable schedules, coordinated scanners or partition owners, dispatch records, bounded workers, attempt state, and reconciliation. Store large versioned payloads separately rather than copying them into every retry message. ¶ Timing control and execution capacity are different paths. Losing the control plane may delay new dispatch while already running work continues. Losing a worker should leave a recoverable run. These behaviours require explicit state and ownership rules, not just a queue attached to a cron loop.

A scanner locks or conditionally claims due state, creates the unique occurrence and dispatch intent, advances next_due, and commits. The worker then claims an attempt epoch and executes under the stable run identity. ¶ It renews a lease where supported and records completion only if still current. A replacement can take over expired work, while effect-side identity or fencing limits harmful stale execution. Reconcile runs whose progress never reaches an acceptable terminal state. This closes the history beyond merely starting a callback.

The assumed 27.8 runs/s and ten-second average yield about 278 active executions. If one worker can sustain four independent I/O jobs, the arithmetic minimum is roughly seventy workers before headroom and other limits. ¶ CPU jobs need measured compute capacity instead. A round-hour burst can be far larger than the average, so estimate due-time distribution and queue delay. Include retries and catch-up. A worker count derived from steady averages is not evidence that launch or outage recovery meets its scheduling objective.

Scheduling delay measures due time to dispatch or start. Runtime measures how long the job takes afterward. An illustrative target is that 95% of eligible runs start within five seconds under supported load. ¶ Scanning intervals, claim coordination, queue waits, cold starts, and concurrency limits all consume that budget. A ten-minute task can still meet a five-second start target, so completion deadlines need another rule. Measure both where the business requires them rather than describing every duration as “job latency.”

Test crashes between occurrence creation and schedule advancement; the atomic boundary should prevent a partial accepted result. Lease expiry can overlap a resumed old worker with a replacement. ¶ Clock changes, timezone transitions, malformed inputs, and poison jobs need policies. Reject stale local completions by epoch. External effects accepted before ownership changes remain a separate reconciliation concern. A timer can be correct about due time while the workflow is still incorrect about identity or repeated execution.

Index due time, claim bounded batches, and partition schedules with stable ownership when scan work becomes limiting. Scale independent workers by their actual CPU, memory, and I/O use. ¶ Keep long, high-priority, and CPU-heavy work from consuming each other's whole budget. Catch-up after an outage also needs limits so millions of overdue runs do not starve current critical jobs. Replacing a global lock requires preserving atomic occurrence creation, not merely removing the contention point and hoping duplicates will be rare.

Advancing the schedule and creating its occurrence need uniqueness and atomicity. Delivery to workers may repeat. Current-epoch completion protects local progress; side-effect identity or enforced fencing protects the relevant external effect. ¶ A lease alone does not establish exactly-once execution. Define missed-run and overlap policy so timing races do not decide which logical runs exist. The accepted schedule, occurrence, attempt, and effect are separate scopes with connected but different consistency requirements.

Authorise schedule ownership, payload references, run views, and cancellation. Validate supported executable work types rather than permitting arbitrary code or unrestricted URLs through the API. ¶ Bind each run to tenant and immutable input. Give the worker only the required capabilities and audit administration. A global administrator credential in every worker would make one malformed or compromised job unnecessarily powerful. Follow access from stored schedule through dispatched task to the actual resource used by the job.

Roll schedule and run formats out compatibly, including lease and epoch meanings. Drain new claims and preserve progress before workers terminate. Reconcile queues against authoritative run state during recovery. ¶ An older restored schedule table can lag external effects already executed. Stable occurrence identity and evidence are needed before replay. Test control-plane interruption separately from worker loss. Recovery must restore the meaning of due work and prior outcomes, rather than simply restart scanning.

Measure due-to-dispatch and due-to-start delay, oldest eligible work, repeated occurrence attempts, lease expiry, stale completion rejection, retries, duration, and tenant fairness. ¶ Check that one occurrence maps to one logical run. CPU can stay low while a broken scanner creates no work, so monitor generation and completion directly. Alert on accepted jobs with no progress. Detailed histories should distinguish one run from its attempts, allowing operators to recognise repetition without interpreting it as new scheduled demand.

Costs include scanning, indexes, history, dispatch, workers, spare capacity, external calls, and recovery. At 2 kB per run and 100,000 runs/hour, raw daily history is 4.8 GB before attempts and indexes. ¶ Compare scan frequency and batching against the start-delay target. A cheaper design that silently misses due runs offers a different service. Use explicit rates and include catch-up and idle headroom so normal execution cost does not hide the work required after a failure.

Polling a due-time index is simple and recoverable. Timer wheels or delayed-message facilities may improve some timing workloads while needing durable reconstruction. Central scanning simplifies ownership; partitioned scanning divides work and adds coordination. ¶ Long-lived workers reduce startup costs, while managed invocation can fit bursts under different limits. Compare timing precision, job duration, recovery, and effects. The timer data structure answers only part of the design; a fast due lookup does not establish safe occurrence or worker semantics.

First implement deterministic time policy and atomic occurrence creation. Add durable dispatch and bounded execution. Introduce leases and epochs together with a safe effect identity before claiming worker takeover. ¶ Partition whichever scan or execution path measurements identify. Regional ownership requires a supported transfer and partition policy for due work. Preserve stable identities throughout so the next deployment does not reinterpret retries as new occurrences or lose the evidence needed to resolve old runs.

Review stable occurrence identity, atomic next_due advancement, stale workers, cancellation, and recovery from old state. Each is a separate potential failure gap. ¶ Run competing scanners at commit boundaries, pause a worker past its lease, cancel during execution, cross a timezone transition, and recover after a long outage. Check which runs should exist and which effects remain uncertain. A callback firing on time once is evidence of timing for one run, not a durable scheduler's full contract.

Use an injected clock and transactional run table. Start two scanners against the same due occurrence and verify that the unique logical run is created once. ¶ Pause an attempt at epoch 7, replace it with epoch 8, then submit the old completion. The local store must reject it. Identify the further key or fencing enforcement needed at an external effect receiver. This separates the local progress guarantee from unsupported claims about physically executing the job once.''',
['Atomic due occurrence creation', 'Durable dispatch of stable run ID', 'Worker claims attempt epoch', 'Conditional completion and reconciliation'],
['Schedule has many occurrences', 'Occurrence identity is UNIQUE', 'Run has many attempts', 'Attempt epoch fences local completion'],
"with transaction():\n    schedule = claim_due_schedule(now)\n    run = insert_unique_run(schedule.id, schedule.next_due)\n    insert_dispatch_outbox(run.id)\n    advance_next_due(schedule)\n# Worker completion compares the current attempt epoch atomically.\n# A stale worker's external effects need resource-side protection.")

case(11, '''Collaborators create a document, edit it together, reconnect after disconnection, and recover required history. They can also export or delete it under access rules. ¶ Choose the editing model: plain text, structured rich text, and embedded objects have different valid operations and conflicts. Cursor position and presence are temporary collaboration hints, separate from durable document content. The first design decision is what an edit means, rather than which transport carries it.

Preserve acknowledged operations and enforce access under a defined revocation policy. Online users need responsive local editing and a bounded supported synchronization delay. ¶ The selected protocol should converge under its assumptions, but equal final strings alone do not establish intended editing behaviour, Unicode correctness, or structural validity. Offline users may return after access changes. Decide what happens to their unaccepted edits and how the interface distinguishes them from accepted history. A local optimistic display is not yet a durable server acknowledgment.

Assume 100,000 active documents, 10,000 edited simultaneously, and three active users per edited document. At two operations/s per user, ingestion is 60,000 operations/s. ¶ Assume 200 retained bytes per operation. That is 12 MB/s, or about 1.037 TB/day if sustained. Actual editing duration, batching, snapshots, metadata, fan-out, and retention change the estimate. A peak concurrency count must not silently become a full-day activity assumption when estimating real storage.

Begin with whole-document updates carrying an expected version. Two conflicting writers cannot both replace the same version; one receives a conflict. This is a reasonable simple contract but an awkward real-time editing experience. ¶ Operation-based synchronization addresses finer-grained concurrent changes. Use a reviewed OT or CRDT model suited to the document structure. Applying arbitrary text patches in arrival order is not enough to establish convergence. The algorithm's operation meaning, context, identity, and recovery rules must work together.

Document creation returns an identity. Sync messages carry document and replica identities, stable operation identifiers, known progress, and protocol version. A snapshot request returns a versioned base with explicit covered history. ¶ Authorise membership changes and exports separately. Clients must not impersonate another replica to alter its operation history. Define acknowledgment and reconnect behaviour so a client knows which edits are accepted, pending, or rejected. A successful transport handshake grants no permission to every later edit or export.

Store document owner and protocol version, membership, accepted operation identities with ordering or causal context, and snapshots naming their covered positions. Compaction metadata records what history can safely be retired. ¶ Sequence CRDTs need stable element identifiers and ordering rules. OT needs the transform context required by its supported offline interval. A snapshot is usable only when the receiver knows which operations it already includes. Dropping that relationship can cause omissions or repeated application even when both snapshot and log files are individually intact.

DocumentService owns identity and membership. SyncCoordinator validates the protocol, EditModel applies its rules, and OperationLog persists accepted work. SnapshotBuilder prepares recovery bases, while Compactor retires only history the protocol permits. ¶ Presence uses a separate temporary path. Keep the deterministic editing core apart from rendering and transport so tests can deliver operations in controlled orders. Each component should expose its progress and ownership rather than interpreting any gateway success as a committed edit.

Use authenticated collaboration gateways, client replicas, durable operation storage, snapshots, and the coordination or merging arrangement required by the chosen algorithm. ¶ OT commonly relies on ordered server context and transformation. CRDT approaches use their defined identities and merge semantics. These are different families, not interchangeable labels on one undefined box. Select one supported protocol per document version and plan any transition. The serving topology must preserve that protocol's persistence and context needs across gateway replacement.

A client applies an allowed local edit optimistically and assigns it stable identity. The server verifies access and protocol context, persists accepted work, and distributes or acknowledges it under the chosen contract. ¶ On reconnect, exchange missing operations or a compatible snapshot and continuation. Ignore repeated operations by identity. Surface rejected offline changes rather than implying they were accepted earlier. This flow keeps local responsiveness separate from durable agreement and access decisions while still giving the user a clear way to recover progress.

The assumed 60,000 operations/s at 200 bytes yields 12 MB/s of raw history. Distribution to collaborators and extra copies add work. One large popular document can concentrate transforms, merge metadata, and gateway traffic. ¶ Snapshots trade replay work for storage and background computation. Measure actual batching, document sizes, offline duration, and tombstone growth. Do not use an average document to size the hottest editor. Independent documents are easy to distribute compared with one shared stream of strongly related operations.

Local optimistic editing can update the screen before a network round trip, while the interface still shows unsynchronised work. Assume 30 ms ingress, 20 ms durable acceptance, and 50 ms peer delivery: the example synchronization path totals 100 ms before additional waiting. ¶ This is not measured p99. Geographic distance, disconnection, and large documents change expectations. Track pending duration separately from client frame responsiveness. A smooth local animation does not establish that an acknowledged edit can be reconstructed after the client disappears.

Two users insert at the same position. The protocol needs a deterministic interpretation. Repeated delivery must not insert the same character twice. Missing history, unsafe compaction, or incompatible snapshots can make reconnect fail. ¶ Test stale membership and resource-heavy malformed operations too. A short online convergence demo does not cover a long partition or deleted metadata. Store enough failing-history context to reproduce problems without logging all confidential content. The recovery assumptions must match the offline behaviour the product advertises.

Distribute independent documents, batch suitable operations, bound gateway buffers, and snapshot to shorten replay. A single hot document may need a specific coordination and fan-out design rather than ordinary hash partitioning. ¶ Compaction must preserve the supported offline and merge horizon. Removing an old identifier because it looks unused can make a returning operation impossible to interpret safely. Scale cursor updates separately from durable edits. They have different retention and correctness needs, even when both use the same live connection.

OT transforms concurrent edits using their relevant ordered context; an insertion can shift another operation's position. A sequence CRDT can use stable element identifiers and a common tie-break rule for concurrent insertions. ¶ If X and Y are inserted after the same element, replicas need compatible rules to reach the intended shared sequence. Neither acronym specifies all rich-text, undo, or user-intent behaviour. Authorisation and durable acknowledgment are separate properties. Choose and test the actual algorithm rather than assuming convergence supplies every document invariant.

Authorise sync sessions and individual operations according to the revocation policy. Long-lived connections and offline clients need explicit membership checks at later boundaries. Restrict exports and snapshots as well as the editor page. ¶ Validate protocol inputs and avoid leaking content through presence or logs. End-to-end encryption changes what the server can search, validate, retain, and revoke; design those consequences if required. TLS secures the transport but does not provide that document-level product guarantee.

Negotiate protocol versions and keep stored history interpretable during rollout. Never reuse an operation identity with a silently changed algorithm. Gateways need reconnect and durable recovery rather than reliance on one client's memory. ¶ Validate snapshots before retiring their source history. Test restoration from persisted artifacts. Protocol migration and rollback affect stored documents, not just application containers, so maintain a supported transition for old clients and offline context. A code rollback that misreads accepted operations can damage otherwise durable content.

Measure pending and accepted edits, synchronization delay, repeated-operation recognition, reconnect gaps, snapshot age, replay duration, compaction growth, protocol rejection, and access denial. ¶ Keep reproducible multi-client histories for failing interleavings. Test convergence alongside access and structural validity. Users' unsynchronised duration reveals harm that gateway latency may miss. Use privacy-safe identifiers and limited diagnostic artifacts rather than copying raw private documents into every trace or log.

Costs include history and snapshots, connection serving, fan-out, protocol computation, compaction, and telemetry. The sustained-rate arithmetic needs replacement with actual activity duration when planning expenditure. ¶ Longer offline support may require more context or metadata. Compare costs at the same supported recovery horizon. Deleting information needed by returning clients makes a cheaper but weaker service. Keep storage savings tied to a valid compaction rule rather than an arbitrary age threshold.

Whole-document version checks suit low collaboration and simple explicit conflicts. OT supports particular ordering and transformation models. CRDTs support specified merging with identifier, metadata, and cleanup costs. ¶ A well-tested editor library may be preferable to a custom algorithm. Evaluate Unicode graphemes, selections, undo, rich-text structure, and offline context as well as final convergence. No family is universally superior; choose the complete semantics required by this document model and the team's ability to verify and maintain them.

Start with versioned documents and clear conflicts. Add a tested operation model, durable history, and reconnect. Then introduce snapshots and justified compaction before distributing independent documents. ¶ Isolate hot workloads and add regional or offline merging only within the protocol's supported assumptions. Preserve stable identities, access policy, and deterministic regression histories. Each stage should retain the meaning of accepted edits; a more distributed editor is not an improvement if old clients can no longer recover their supported context.

Review convergence, offline duration, history retirement, and revocation. Deliver concurrent inserts in different orders, repeat a delete, lose an acknowledgment, and reconnect from an old snapshot plus later operations. ¶ Also reconnect a revoked user and inspect structural validity. Equal strings alone can conceal an unauthorised edit or invalid document structure. Check those properties separately. The review should name the protocol and assumptions under which each claimed result follows.

Implement a miniature merge of two insertions with stable IDs and an explicit tie-break rule. Deliver them in both orders and repeat each. Verify the same final sequence and no duplicate insertion. ¶ Document the limited scope: this is not a complete rich-text CRDT. Add snapshot-context and revoked-client checks to expose other boundaries. The exercise teaches why identity and deterministic ordering matter, without treating a two-operation example as evidence for a full production editor.''',
['Authorized local edit with identity', 'Protocol orders or merges operations', 'Durable log acknowledges accepted work', 'Snapshot and missing-operation recovery'],
['Document owns protocol version', 'Operation has stable ID and context', 'Snapshot records covered progress', 'Membership applies to every sync'],
'def receive(operation, document):\n    with transaction():\n        current = lock_document(document.id)\n        require_authorized(operation.actor, current)\n        require_protocol_version(operation, current)\n        if log.contains(operation.id): return prior_ack(operation.id)\n        next_state = edit_model.apply(current.state, operation)\n        append_unique_operation(operation)\n        commit_state_and_progress(current, next_state)\n    return durable_ack(operation.id)\n# The edit model must implement a specified, tested algorithm.')

case(12, '''A tenant uploads a document, the system classifies it and extracts fields, and uncertain results go to human review. Validated output can be published for authorised search or export. The user can inspect progress, correct results, reprocess versions, and request deletion. ¶ Successful upload means accepted bytes under the upload contract. It does not mean extraction, review, or publication has finished. Give these stages distinct states so users can understand what is ready and what remains unresolved.

Preserve accepted inputs and required workflow progress under the chosen failures. Results must identify their exact source and pipeline versions. Protect tenant data and set processing-age objectives for supported work. ¶ Do not silently turn uncertain extraction into verified fact. Human decisions need durable actor and rationale information. Search has its own publication delay and access obligations. A complete result is usable, traceable, and permitted output, rather than a worker merely setting one finished flag.

Assume one million files/day averaging 2 MB: raw input is 2 TB/day. The arrival average is about 11.6 files/s; a tenfold assumed peak is about 116/s. ¶ At an illustrative five CPU-seconds per file, daily extraction work is five million CPU-seconds, or about 1,389 CPU-hours. Classification, I/O, retries, and human wait are additional work. Actual model behaviour and file distribution need measurement. These quantities teach resource accounting and do not assert an extraction benchmark or accuracy rate.

A prototype can save a file and extract it during the upload request. Large or difficult inputs then extend the request, process loss can erase progress, and human review has no natural place to wait. ¶ Separate resumable byte ingestion from durable workflow stages. Keep immutable source versions and identified outputs. Retries and corrections can then preserve the evidence needed to explain earlier results. The improvement addresses a concrete recovery and lifecycle problem before adding workers for capacity.

POST /documents creates an upload intent, and completion verifies stored bytes. Status exposes stages; field reads return versioned values and provenance. Review decisions, versioned reprocessing, and tracked deletion have separate operations. ¶ Authorise search and export only over permitted published output. Explain whether deletion is accepted or fully completed, and what a reprocessing request changes. A request for a new pipeline version must not silently reinterpret old output under the same identity.

Document identity binds tenant, source version, digest, and state. Upload sessions track bytes; workflows identify input and pipeline versions. Stage attempts carry epochs, artifacts carry kind/version/object references, and fields carry values, confidence, source regions, and extractor identity. ¶ Review records identify decision, actor, and version. Publication and deletion have tracked progress. Keys preserve tenant and version scope. Retain enough provenance to audit a result, with an explicit lifecycle rather than assuming every raw intermediate should remain forever.

UploadService and ContentValidator check ingestion. WorkflowCoordinator owns stages. Versioned Classifier and Extractor adapters produce results; ProvenanceRecorder links evidence. ReviewPolicy selects the supported review path, ReviewService records decisions, and Publisher builds permitted projections. ¶ Reconciler repairs stuck or orphaned work. Workers receive limited capabilities and cannot change tenant identity because a message supplied a different field. This division keeps model output generation separate from deciding whether it is valid, reviewed, and ready for others to use.

Use immutable object storage, durable relational workflow metadata, task delivery, bounded isolated compute or model workers, review interfaces, and a search projection. Bulk bytes flow through scoped storage access while metadata controls progress. ¶ A document waiting hours for review should release its compute slot and database transaction. The workflow remains durable without keeping its process alive. Keep source, artifacts, human decisions, and publication generations linked so failure in one component can be investigated and repaired.

Verify upload identity and digest, create a stable workflow, classify, extract, and validate structure and meaning. Save evidence, then either publish or request review. A human decision creates a new validated version and publication intent. ¶ Each stage commits conditionally under its current attempt epoch. Reprocessing creates a new run over an identified input, preserving earlier provenance. Deletion blocks later inappropriate publication. This sequence makes resumed or stale workers safe locally instead of relying on the assumption that only one attempt ever runs.

Thirty days of 2 TB/day raw input requires 60 TB before extra copies. Five million CPU-seconds/day divided by 86,400 gives about 57.9 continuously busy cores. At an assumed 60% target utilisation, that becomes about 96.5 cores before other work and failure margin. ¶ A tenfold arrival peak can exceed this average capacity. Queue age, scaling delay, admission, and peak duration determine whether processing objectives still hold. Include large-file and model-memory distribution rather than treating every input as the average file.

Upload transfer and durable acceptance have one timeline. Automated processing includes queueing, classification, extraction, validation, and publication. Human review is a separate waiting state. ¶ Assume two seconds queued, one second classification, five seconds extraction, and one second validation/publication. The automated path totals nine seconds in this model. These are testable assumptions, not measured model performance. Report the relevant stage and age so a user can distinguish slow computation from a pending human decision.

Uploads can be incomplete, inputs corrupt or hostile, and workers interrupted after writing output but before saving completion. Repeated tasks, stale attempts, invalid model output, concurrent reviews, and missing provenance can each break a different part of the workflow. ¶ Use stable artifact identity and conditional transitions. Quarantine unsupported output with a reason. Check deletion during processing. A completed label with missing, unauthorised, or unexplained output is still a failed business result even when no worker exception was recorded.

Move bulk bytes through direct storage and scale independent extraction with bounded workers. Partition metadata by a justified tenant or workflow key while protecting against large files and hot tenants. ¶ Model batching and memory limits need explicit testing. More workers can saturate storage or an external model. Give replay and backfill separate capacity so pipeline upgrades do not starve live inputs or deletion. Scale according to the stage that limits useful output rather than multiplying every worker equally.

Source versions are immutable. Related workflow and review changes need suitable atomic transitions. Artifacts identify their exact input and transform, while repeated stage delivery uses stable output identity and current-epoch completion. ¶ Search may lag publication while respecting access. External model calls can be uncertain or nondeterministic. Retain relevant request and version information, validate results, and state the model's actual reproducibility boundary. Recording a model name alone does not establish that rerunning it will yield the same answer.

Enforce tenant scope on objects, tasks, review, search, export, and audit paths. Restrict worker resources and egress, validate supported content, and keep credentials and private data out of routine telemetry. ¶ Treat extracted text as untrusted content, not infrastructure instructions. Reviewers need scoped access and traceable decisions. Deletion covers derived sensitive results as well as raw bytes. A protected upload endpoint is only the first boundary in a pipeline that repeatedly reads and republishes the document's information.

Version pipelines and schemas compatibly. Evaluate new models on authorised samples or controlled cohorts and review quality before replacing published output. Retain the previous usable generation until the transition is verified. ¶ Drain workers through durable attempts. Restore exercises must reconnect objects, workflow state, human decisions, and deletion history. Restoring only the metadata database may leave missing artifacts or revive obsolete publication. Test the recovered whole journey before treating it as a usable platform.

Measure accepted bytes, stage waiting, computation, retries, stale-completion rejection, invalid outputs, review age, provenance completeness, publication lag, tenant denials, and deletion progress. ¶ Confidence is not measured accuracy. Use an appropriate labelled evaluation for quality claims. Link source, run, attempt, model, artifact, and reviewer through stable identities in privacy-safe evidence. This lets an operator find where one document stopped and whether its result is actually usable, rather than merely observe a busy worker fleet.

Include raw and derived storage, CPU or model calls, retries, review labour, search, transfer, telemetry, and recovery. The assumed five million CPU-seconds/day is a compute input, while review may dominate actual operating cost. ¶ Use explicit unit rates and a stated review fraction when comparing choices. Avoid invented accuracy or throughput numbers. Compare equivalent provenance, access, quality, and recovery needs; dropping review or history may reduce the bill while changing the service's required outcome.

Synchronous processing suits a tiny demonstration but couples upload to model delay and failure. Durable stages add metadata and operating work while supporting recovery and human waits. Batch inference may improve resource use while increasing waiting and memory needs. ¶ Managed workflows or models transfer selected duties but do not automatically provide provenance, validation, tenant isolation, or review policy. Compare the full output and recovery contract. The simplest viable architecture is the one that explains and supports these outcomes for the intended workload.

Start with immutable upload and explicit status. Add durable stages and stable artifacts before increasing parallelism. Give provenance and human decisions first-class identities rather than attaching them after extraction. ¶ Then scale workers and storage independently, and introduce authorised search and versioned reprocessing. Regional placement needs a named locality or disaster requirement and a compatible lifecycle design. Every stage should keep prior results explainable and prevent old work from bypassing current access or deletion decisions.

Check whether any stage silently changes its input version, whether a superseded worker can publish after deletion, and whether reprocessing preserves human decisions appropriately. ¶ Test repeated tasks, interruption after artifact write, invalid output, concurrent reviews, stale completion, missing objects, index lag, and full restoration. A few correct extracted fields in one run do not establish recoverable progress or provenance. The review needs evidence for the connected lifecycle, including work that ends in rejection, review, or deletion.

Implement three stages using immutable input identity, result version, and attempt epoch. Stop after writing an artifact but before recording completion, then replay without duplicating the established output. ¶ Add a review state that releases compute resources while waiting. Finally pause an old worker, delete the document, and submit that worker's completion. Publication must remain blocked. Explain how the persisted condition prevents stale work from reversing deletion rather than relying on worker timing.''',
['Verified immutable upload', 'Durable versioned extraction workflow', 'Validation and human review', 'Authorized publication with provenance'],
['Document owns source versions', 'Workflow owns stage attempts', 'Field links source region and model', 'Review and deletion are durable state'],
'def complete_stage(run, epoch, artifact):\n    require(artifact.input_version == run.input_version)\n    validate_output_schema_and_provenance(artifact)\n    with transaction():\n        current = lock_workflow(run.id)\n        require(current.epoch == epoch and not current.deleted)\n        insert_artifact_once(run.id, artifact.version, artifact)\n        advance_or_request_review(current, artifact)\n        insert_next_stage_outbox_if_needed(current)\n# Retry reuses identities; old attempts cannot publish new state.')

